Apart from the above three classes of phylogenetic networks, two more will play an important role in this paper. For the definition of these two, we need the notion of a {\it reticulation cycle} (or {\it gall}) which is a set of two paths from a common top tree vertex to a common bottom reticulation, with the sets of internal vertices being disjoint. Then, we have the following definitions:
\begin{itemize}
\item A {\it galled network} is a phylogenetic network with each reticulation in exactly one reticulation cycle.
\item A {\it galled tree} is a galled network whose reticulation cycles are edge-disjoint.
\end{itemize}
Fig.~\ref{fig2} illustrates these two classes of networks.
