	\title{Fast offline decoding with local message-passing automata}

	\author[]{\fontsize{12pt}{16pt}\selectfont \fnm{Ethan} \sur{Lake}}

	\affil[]{\small Department of Physics, University of California Berkeley, Berkeley, CA 94720}


	\abstract{
		We present a local offline decoder for topological codes that operates according to a parallelized message-passing framework. The decoder works by passing messages between anyons, with the contents of received messages used to move nearby anyons towards one another. We prove the existence of a threshold, and show that in a system of linear size \normalmath{$L$}, decoding terminates with an \normalmath{$O((\log L)^\eta)$} average-case runtime, where \normalmath{$\eta$} is a small constant. For i.i.d Pauli noise, our decoder has \normalmath{$\eta=1$} and a threshold at a noise strength of \normalmath{$p_c\approx 7.3\%$}. 
	}

	\maketitle

