\section{Discussion} \label{seq:discussion}
In this tutorial, we illustrated how to perform statistical estimation in cost-effectiveness analysis with observational data.

We focused on the suBART model, both due to personal affinity with it, and because it has been shown to perform well in the aforementioned use case. 
%We stress, however, that the presented framework is quite general and works with many different statistical estimators for causal inference. 
We stress, however, that our main intention is not a presentation of this model, but rather to provide more applied cost-effectiveness researchers a more general framework for analysing observational data. suBART is merely an example, and there are alternatives which can be used in a similar way, and which we also expect to perform well. In principle, any non-Bayesian method for estimating $\Delta_c$ and $\Delta_q$ could be used, in conjunction with bootstrapping. The MCMC sample in Section \ref{code:MCMC_sample} would then be replaced by the bootstrap sample, and the rest of the analysis proceeds in the same way. Such an analysis is no longer Bayesian, and hence the interpretation of the results changes: strictly speaking, we can no longer speak of the "probability of cost-effectiveness", and must instead argue in terms of frequentist p-values. See \citet{lothgren2000definition} for details. Also, as mentioned earlier, such approaches may be infeasible in practice, due to computational constraints.
Suitable choices for the estimator could include, for example, the Causal Forest of \citet{wager2018estimation} or Double Machine Learning \citep{knaus2022double}.

We now discuss some limitations of our presented approach (which are however shared by the aforementioned alternatives) as well as possible remedies.

\begin{itemize}
\item 
suBART can only deal with missing observations in the outcome variables, not in the covariates. The technical reason for this is that the model is formulated conditional on the covariates, and hence makes no assumptions about the distribution of the covariates. In principle this is a good thing, because the analysis is therefore not dependent on such assumptions, but it also means that there is no way to impute missing covariates. 
Conceptually it seems most appealing to directly impute the missing covariates using the BART machinery, as proposed by \citet{xu2016sequential}. While applying this idea to suBART is conceptually straightforward, a computational implementation is currently not available. As a pragmatic alternative, we suggest combining suBART with multiple imputation in the same way as is commonly done when performing CEAs with trial data. The procedure can be sketched as follows: we create multiple complete dataset using standard imputation techniques and compute, for each of these datasets, the point estimates and covariance matrix of $\Delta_c$ and $\Delta_q$ (see Listing \ref{code:MCMC_sample}). These are then pooled using Rubin's rules, and the pooled estimates may be used to construct the CEAC. In this context we refer to \citet{harel2007multiple} for a general review of multiple imputation, and  \citet{ben2023conducting} for specific guidance on imputation in trial-based CEAs.

\item
We focussed on the basic setting in observational studies, where the treatment for each patient is assigned once and then not changed anymore. Most cost-effectiveness studies in the literature are of this kind, but there is also increasing interest in performing CEAs with so called time-varying treatments [add reference]. In such analyses, the patients may switch from one treatment to another during follow-up. The probability of such switches happening may depend on confounders, and these confounders may themselves change over time - this is accordingly called time-varying confounding. Statistical adjustment for such confounding is quite complicated; \citet{hernan2024causal} devote about a third of their book to it. One of their proposed methods, called the \emph{g-formula for time-varying treatments} (Section 21.1) could in principle be combined with BART, and some first efforts in this direction have been made by \citet{wang2024longbet}. Extending their work to the CEA setting seems possible in principle, but far beyond the scope of the present paper. 


\end{itemize}

