%-------------------------------------------------- Abstract ---------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{strip}
    \begin{ecoc_abstract}
        We measured the outage probability of a 4.6-km urban free-space optical communication link over six days. High-speed power measurements reveal slow and fast fading effects, with link availabilities of 92\% including and 99\% excluding slow fades for 500 Gb/s transmission. \textcopyright2025 The Author(s) \vspace{-3.5mm}
    \end{ecoc_abstract}
\end{strip}

%-------------------------------------------------- Introduction Section -------------------------------------------------------%
\section{Introduction}%
\vspace*{-1mm}
Terrestrial \FSO communication links have been shown to accommodate multi-terabit-per-second data transmission over multi-kilometre wireless urban channels \cite{vanvliet202557tbstransmission46}. Combining the flexibility of wireless communication systems with the high capacity of fibre-optic transmission technologies, \FSO communications is attractive for applications such as backhaul for cellular networks, campus connectivity, and disaster recovery \cite{SurveyOnFSO}. When operated with commonly used optical frequencies, such as the C-band, mature high-quality fibre-optic components enable seamless integration with existing fibre-optic infrastructure. However, key to the successful adaptation of terrestrial \FSO communications, even in hybrid systems, is a thorough understanding of the reliability and availability of these communication links. Previous experimental studies provide initial insights\cite{10.1117/12.338979, e90-c_2_381, polimifirstresults, aveiro4tbpsjlt}, but were performed over short distances, over brief periods of time, and/or with low data rates.   

\begin{figure*}[!b]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/Photonloop_ECOC_2025_setup.pdf}
    \caption{Experimental setup for coherent \sFSO data transmission with 3 \sWDM channels over \qty{4661}{\m} across Eindhoven with the elevation profile directly underneath the \FSO channel w.r.t. Amsterdam Ordnance Datum. Insets show spectra of the transmitted (a) and received (b) signal, a map illustrating the link's path across the city centre, and photographs of the installed optical terminals.}
    \label{fig:setup}
\end{figure*}

In this work, we measure the transmission performance of a coherent communication signal over a \qty{4.6}{\km} urban \FSO channel for over six days, during which the weather ranges from clear sky to thick fog. We evaluate the outage probability and corresponding availability by analysing the decoded data rate. Furthermore, we correlate the data transmission performance with simultaneous high-speed measurements of the power coupled into the receiver fibre and with visibility measurements from a nearby weather station. We differentiate between fast and slow fades to investigate the respective effects on the outage probability. We find that deep slow fades are the primary contributor to the outage probability, in the worst case completely prohibiting data transmission. We observe a significantly smaller impact from fast fades, achieving \qty{500}{Gb/s} data transmission with an overall availability of \qty{99}{\%} outside of deep slow fades.

%-------------------------------------------------- Experimental Setup Section ———————————————————————————%
\vspace*{-3mm}
\section{Experimental setup}
\cref{fig:setup} shows the experimental setup with a 3-channel coherent \WDM system and the urban \qty{4.6}{\km} \FSO link. In the coherent \WDM transmitter, three \qty{50}{GHz}-spaced \WDM channels were generated by modulating three \ECLs in two \DPIQs. A 4-channel \SI{100}{GSa/s} \AWG generating a \SI{48}{GBd} \qam{4} signal, filtered with a \lRRC having a roll-off of 0.01, modulated both \DPIQs. The transmitted spectrum is shown in \cref{fig:setup}a. A booster \EDFA amplified the signals to \qty{30}{dBm} before the light was collimated to an eye-safe\cite{iec60825:2014} free-space beam in the transmitter optical terminal.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/Photonloop_ECOC_2025_results.pdf}
    \caption{(a) Mean received optical power per minute. (b) Horizontal visibility at ground level measured at Eindhoven Aiport. (c) Rytov variance ($\sigma^2_l$) per minute. (d) Decoded data rate for all \qty{74890}{} \RTO captures and their mean per \qty{15}{minutes}.}
    \label{fig:results}
\end{figure*}

Fibre-coupled optical terminals, prototypes of commercial devices from the development partner Aircision, are employed to establish the urban \FSO link of \qty{4.6}{\km}. Spanning the city of Eindhoven, the Netherlands, the link is part of the permanent Reid Photonloop \FSO test bed and connects the campus of Eindhoven University of Technology with the High Tech Campus. \Cref{fig:setup} shows the elevation profile directly underneath the \FSO channel together with a map illustrating the position of the link with respect to the city centre. Inset are photographs of the installed terminals and a view of the receiver site seen from the transmitter site through binoculars.

An automated tracking and pointing system in the optical terminals provides continuous optimisation of the free-space-to-fibre coupling. To further mitigate fluctuations in the received optical power, a pre-amplifier \EDFA was operated in continuous output power mode at the receiver \cite{vanVliet_ECOC:24}. A \WSS was used to sequentially select one of the three \WDM channels from the amplified signal, after which it was mixed with a \LO in a coherent receiver. The electrical outputs were digitised for \SI{10}{\micro s} per capture using a 4-channel \SI{80}{GS/s} \RTO, taking 25 captures before switching to the next \WDM channel. A short duration is required between the measurement of each \WDM channel to tune the \LO and \WSS. Offline \lDSP, mainly consisting of a decision-directed \lMIMO equalizer with an in-loop \lBPS algorithm, was used to recover the transmitted signal \cite{MennoThesis}. Metrics such as \lGMI were determined to evaluate the transmission performance, together with a more practical decoding scheme combining \lLDPC codes from the DVB-S2 standard with code-rate puncturing and an additional hard-decision outer code to obtain error-free transmission \cite{rademacher_peta-bit-per-second_2021}.

Simultaneously to data transmission, a fraction of the optical power before the pre-amplifier \EDFA was tapped to continuously measure the power in the receiver fibre with a \qty{10}{kS/s} power meter (PM). In addition, an \OSA was employed to capture the received spectrum. Moreover, a tap after the optical pre-amplifier and an optical switch enabled measurements of the amplified output power and spectrum.


%-------------------------------------------------- Results Section ———————————————————————————%
\section{Results}
Results from over six days of continuous measuring are presented in \cref{fig:results}. Ranging from October 24, 2024, 17:34 till October 30, 2024, 21:00, \qty{74890}{} \RTO captures were obtained during these \qty{8846}{minutes}, or over \qty{147}{hours}. \Cref{fig:results}a shows the mean fibre-coupled optical power per minute, an indicator of slowly evolving atmospheric effects. Significant fluctuations are observed, with deep slow fades resulting in a complete loss of optical signal power on October 27, 29, and 30. During these days, we witnessed minimal visibility due to fog and heavy rain. To provide a quantitative measure for this, the horizontal visibility at ground level measured at Eindhoven Airport, located outside the city at approximately \qty{7}{\km} from the \FSO channel under investigation, is plotted in \cref{fig:results}b. Due to the spatial separation between the weather station and the \FSO test bed, there is a slight difference between the measured visibility and the concurrent conditions in the investigated \FSO channel. Nevertheless, the data indicates extremely limited visibility in the Eindhoven area during the periods with deep slow fades.

\begin{figure}[!t]%
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/Photonloop_ECOC_2025_outage_probability_unfiltered.pdf}
    \caption{Measured outage probability and availability versus decoded data rate and code rate per day and over all days.}
    \label{fig:out_prob_unfil}
\end{figure}

Information on fast fades is extracted from the power meter measurements by fitting it to a log-normal model to calculate the Rytov variance ($\sigma^2_l$) per minute \cite{ghassemlooy2012optical}. The Rytov variance is a measure of irradiance fluctuations, mainly caused by the atmosphere and the automatic alignment system's residual tracking error, with a larger Rytov variance corresponding to stronger fluctuations. For irradiance fluctuations induced by atmospheric turbulence, $\sigma^2_l \leq 1$ is typically considered as weak-to-moderate optical turbulence, while $\sigma^2_l > 1$ indicates strong irradiance fluctuations \cite{Andrews2005LaserEditionb}. As the method for determining the Rytov variance requires received signal power, it is not plotted during the deep slow fades mentioned earlier.

The decoded data rate $R_{dec}$ for all \qty{74890}{} \RTO captures is displayed in \cref{fig:results}d. By taking the mean per 15 minutes, it becomes clear that, as expected, link outages occur during the deep slow fades with complete loss of optical signal power. Outside these periods, the vast majority of the captured signals are decoded successfully. By defining the outage probability for a given decoded data rate $R$ as $P_{out}=P(R_{dec}\leq R)$, we can plot the outage probability and corresponding availability versus decoded data rate and corresponding code rate, as shown in \cref{fig:out_prob_unfil}. The days with deep slow fades can be discerned by the elevated plateaus, with October 30 having an outage probability of \qty{25.44}{\%} for $R$=\qty{500}{Gb/s}, for example. When taking all \RTO captures into account, the corresponding overall outage probability for the \textgreater6 days is \qty{8.37}{\%}. It becomes evident that deep slow fading is the primary contributor to this when we remove the \RTO captures obtained during minutes with complete loss of signal power, as plotted in \cref{fig:out_prob_filt}. Here, we see that for October 30, the outage probability for $R$=\qty{500}{Gb/s} is \qty{0.40}{\%} outside of deep slow fades. Similarly, the overall outage probability drops to \qty{1.06}{\%}. As expected, corresponding outage probabilities for days without deep slow fades are equal in both figures, for example \qtylist{0.37;0.67;0.57}{\%} for October 24, 25, and 26, respectively.

These results highlight the two-sided nature of availability and reliability in terrestrial \FSO communication links. Fast fading increases the outage probability through short outages. These detrimental effects on data transmission performance can be mitigated by using techniques for optimising free-space-to-fibre coupling, such as the automated tracking and pointing system used here, and link adaptation techniques, such as adaptive coding, error correction, retransmission and interleaving. For example, in this work the overall outage probability outside of deep slow fades can be reduced by \qty{43}{\%} by operating with a code rate of \qty{0.52}{} instead of \qty{0.87}{}, at the cost of transmission rate. On the other hand, deep slow fading can cause a complete loss of optical signal power for extended periods, rendering data communication impossible. With limited available transmit power due to eye-safety requirements, it is impossible to mitigate this effect in a single link. This underscores the need for integration of terrestrial \FSO links in a hybrid network, if high availability is required.

\begin{figure}[!t]%
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/Photonloop_ECOC_2025_outage_probability_filtered.pdf}
    \caption{Measured outage probability and availability versus decoded data rate and code rate for data transmission captured outside of deep slow fades, per day and over all days.}
    \label{fig:out_prob_filt}
\end{figure}

%-------------------------------------------------- Conclusions Section ———————————————————————————%
\section{Conclusions and outlook}
We measured the transmission performance of a coherent communication signal over a \qty{4.6}{\km} urban \FSO channel for over six days. We find that deep slow fades are the primary contributor to the outage probability. In the future we plan to expand the Reid Photonloop \FSO test bed with dedicated instruments for atmospheric measurements to enable more in-depth and longer-term investigation of the urban \FSO channel and its availability and reliability.

