\begin{abstract}
We report the observation of a bunching of $\sim$3 photon states, which is a coherent superposition of emitted photons and resonantly scattered laser photons, arising upon excitation by even-$\pi$ pulses of a two-level system represented by a charged quantum dot in a microcavity. This phenomenon emerges because the exciting laser pulse contains several tens of photons whose quantum amplitude distribution creates such a superposition, and the polarization of the scattered photons is changed by the interaction with the charged resonant system. Such a beam is a high-order member of the Fock space, promising for quantum technologies.

%When a two-level system, a charged quantum dot in a microcavity, is excited by even-$\pi$ pulses, we observe, despite strict cross-polarization filtering, a bunch of $\sim$3 photon states, which is a coherent superposition of the emitted photons and resonantly scattered laser photons, which changes at energy detuning. The theory is based on the fact that the excitation pulse contains several tens of photons, the quantum amplitude distribution of which creates such a superposition, and that the polarization of photons is controlled by coupling with the charged resonant system. Such a bunch is a high-order member of the Fock space, promising for quantum technology.
\end{abstract} 

