The analysis increment of TCWV (Fig.~\ref{fig:TCWV}c) has an elliptic shape with a greater extent in the zonal than in the meridional direction, reflecting the dominance of the zonal flow in the tropical atmosphere. A negative increment in T2m is also present (Fig.~\ref{fig:TCWV}d). This may indicate latent cooling due to evaporation of precipitation in the lowest layers -- once again implicitly captured by the autoencoder.
%We can speculate with a possible explanation for this phenomenon: the $\mathbf{B}_z$-matrix understands that the presence of a cloud means precipitation that cools the air near the ground. It is worth noting that the ST increment had no distinctive pattern in the area of the cloud and was much weaker than T2m increment (not shown).

Does our DA and the associated latent-space $\mathbf{B}_z$-matrix capture the response to latent-heat-induced perturbations in the tropics? To provide further evidence, we analyze the vertical profile of horizontal divergence in the analysis increment. Figure~\ref{fig:TCWV}e shows that, at the location of the observed TCWV, the convergent wind increment is present in the lower troposphere, peaking around 900\,hPa. Near 500\,hPa, the flow transitions to divergent, which peaks at 200\,hPa, with a magnitude twice as large as the maximum convergence. This vertical structure closely resembles observational findings from \citet{Williams1973StatisticalPacific}, suggesting that our system realistically represents the dynamical response to tropical latent heating. This highlights a remarkable capability of the latent-space background-error covariance model -- one not yet demonstrated by any other variational DA system.

%where in the mesoscale convective cloud system the convergence typically peaks between 950\,hPa and 900\,hPa, it transverses to divergence at approximately 400\,hPa and the divergence peaks between 250\,hPa and 150\,hPa with its maximum magnitude being two to four times the maximum magnitude of the convergence, depending on the scenario. We, therefore, find it likely that what we see truly resembles the humidity saturation in the tropics.


