To obtain the distribution of variable $C$, we  calculate the variance in occupation, $\sigma_{C}(s)^2 = \langle (C - \bar{C} )^2 \rangle $. An estimate of this variance can be obtained from the observation that the regulatory region is either occupied or unoccupied during each measurement; in time $\tau$, there will be of order $N \approx \tau/ \tau_c$ independent measurements, where the correlation time 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:tauc}
\tau_c = \frac{1}{k_{\textrm {off}} + k_{\textrm {on}} s^h}
\end{equation} can be obtained from the master equation. Therefore, we expect a binomial variance of order $\sigma_{C}(s)^2 \approx \frac{1}{N} \bar{C}(1 - \bar{C})$, where in steady state $\bar{C} (1 -\bar{C})$ corresponds to the instantaneous variance \cite{Kaizu, tenWolde, bialekbook}. We follow the calculation in \cite{Kaizu} to obtain the exact result (Appendix \ref{sec:appA}), 
\begin{align} \label{eq:var3}
\sigma_{\bar{C}}(s)^2  =\frac{2 \tau_c }{\tau}  \bar{C} (1- \bar{C}) \textrm{,}
\end{align}
 in agreement with \cite{Kaizu, tenWolde, bialekbook}. This differs from the ad-hoc expectation by a factor of two; this factor is required when the concentration $s$ is inferred from the occupation during the time interval $\tau$, whereas inferring the concentration from the discrete number of (independent) binding events allows for a lower variance \cite{EndresWingreen}.


The standard deviation in occupation (Eq. \ref{eq:var3}) increases with the correlation time of the occupation, which in turn increases with Hill coefficient $h$ (Eq. \ref{eq:tauc}). Therefore, one might expect that cooperativity in the form of $h>1$ reduces the information transfer, consistent with Refs \cite{BialekSetayeshgar2,SkogeWingreen, SkogeII}.

We can now calculate how precisely a binding site sensor can measure signal concentration $s$, provided the sensor is determined by parameters $h$ and $k$ and has access to a time-averaged occupation during a particular interval $\tau$. We calculate the distribution for the occupation $P(C|s)$  using Eqs. \ref{eq:hill}, \ref{eq:var3} and \ref{eq:gauss}. We investigate two representative measurement times  for a short and long measurement $\tau$. The long measurement corresponds approximately to the duration of nc 13 (10 min), provided that transcription factor residence times of order $\approx 1$ s  can set an effective timescale for  $k_{\textrm {off}}\sim 1/\text{s}$. The short measurement is still longer than the correlation time, to ensure that the Gaussian approximation is valid; we chose a time of approximately $\tau=10$ s.

Both the information about the signal $I(C;s)$ as well as the information of relevance to the embryo, $I(C;x)$ show a clear maximum at a specific parameter combination of $h$ and $k$ per measurement time, similar to earlier work that also incorporated noise in RNA production \cite{tkavcik2009optimizing} (Fig. \ref{fig:1}C for $\tau=10$ s, Appendix \ref{sec:appA} for a more detailed discussion and $\tau =10$ min). For each value of cooperativity $h$, more information can maximally be captured over $k$ for longer $\tau$ (Fig. \ref{fig:1}D) %for the maximal information over $k$ with a constraint on $h$ for two representative $\tau$, Fig. \ref{fig:1}E for maximized information over both parameters). 
Even for the longer $\tau$, the optimal number of cooperative binding sites is larger than one, suggesting that on realistic timescales some cooperativity may be optimal. 

%Similarly, binding site parameters can be optimized to maximize the information of relevance to the embryo $I(C;x)$ that the site captures, with a clear maximum in $I(C;x)$ at specific parameters $h$ and $k$ for constant $\tau$ (Fig. \ref{fig:1}C, Appendix \ref{sec:appA} Fig. 5). Looking at the value of $h$ at the optima in Fig. \ref{fig:1}D, $h \approx 2.1$ and $h \approx 4.8$, we observe that a finite level of cooperativity is optimal for both measurement times and that the optima occur at higher values of $h$ than for $I(C;s)$.

We note that $I(C;x)$ saturates at approximately $1.8$ bits, as the binding site cannot extract more information about cell fates than the signal Bicoid itself provides (additional signals would allow for more information \cite{Dubuis}). 
For long $\tau$, a range of cooperativities $h$ achieves the optimum (exact optimum positions at $h \approx 2.1$ and $h \approx 4.8$). Even if we compare this measurement to one where $h$ binding sites are treated independently, the information could not be higher than $1.8$ bits. 

%If there was a constraint on a high number of binding sites $h>8$, longer measurement times would not necessarily improve $I(C;x)$(Fig. \ref{fig:1}D). 

Both $I(C;s)$ and $I(C;x)$ increase with $\tau$ when optimized over $h$ and $k$ (Fig. \ref{fig:1}E). 
The optimal number of cooperatively binding molecules $h$, maximizing $I(C;x)$, decreases with measurement time (Fig. \ref{fig:1}E bottom). For increasing $\tau \to \infty$, it saturates at  values $h\approx 1.38$; and remains larger than two even at  measurement times of $1000$ s. 

From an information-theoretic perspective, it makes sense that steep thresholds or high $h$ are optimal for shorter measurement times,  associated with high noise or variance in $P(C|s)$:  binary, threshold-like measurements are known to provide the most information when noise is high \cite{Baueretal, Mattingly2018}. For longer $\tau$  and more precise measurements, we find that weak cooperativity is optimal or does not reduce information up until $h<3$. This has two reasons: first, $h=1$ does not lead to a linear $\bar{C}$, which makes intuition difficult. Second, for considering $I(C;x)$, we should note that the Bicoid gradient itself is not a linear input; we know from rate-distortion theory that an optimal map $\bar{C}$ that reads a variable $x$ that is conveyed through a signal with a non-linear mean ($\bar{s}(x)$) should itself be a nonlinear function of the signal (concretely $\bar{C}(s) =\bar{x}(s)$ \cite{CoverThomas}). Since optimizing $I(C;x)$ is additionally convex, different non-linear maps can achieve the optimum. 
%it is Since cooperativity allows for creating nonlinear occupation functions $\bar{C}$, we should not expect the absence of cooperativity to be optimal when we assume that the binding site needs to capture information about a variable of relevance beyond just reading the signal itself.

If the longer measurement time of 10 minutes is biologically realistic for the fly embryo, an optimally adjusted binding site sensor would have approximately 2-3 cooperatively binding molecules. Interestingly, this does indeed correspond to the number of strongly binding Bcd molecules in the hbP2 enhancer \cite{Park_2019}. We do not expect an exact match, as our estimates for timescales are approximative, but are encouraged to find a reasonable estimate for $h$. We conclude that our heuristic Hill-function model suggests that some cooperativity is optimal, consistent with information-theoretical expectations considering the functional form of the signal. Yet, Hill-function model suffers from the significant shortcoming that we are forced to consider longer measurement times and cannot capture the binding and unbinding of individual molecules. To check the role of clustering in more detail, we next investigate the role of clustering of transcription factors around binding sites with a more realistic mechanistic model.
