\section{The groups we study and the tools we use}\label{sec:preliminaries}

This section contains some elementary lemmas and is used to set the stage for the proof of our main theorem. In \Cref{sec:coarse-geometry} we extend the conjugator length function to the class of locally compact topological groups, collect some elementary properties of split subgroups of $\Isom(\E^n)$ in \Cref{sec:mainsetup}, recall geometric results on conjugation from \cite{MST4} in \Cref{sec:coconjugation-sets}, and finally cite from the literature some basic facts on pseudoinverses in \Cref{sec:pseudoinverse}. 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Conjugator length and coarse geometry of locally compact groups}
\label{sec:coarse-geometry}

Interest in the conjugator length function for discrete (finitely generated) groups has been rapidly increasing in recent years. Here we briefly note that, similarly to Dehn functions, conjugator length is equally as meaningful to locally compact groups, so let us provide definitions. We disclaim that all topological groups we treat are Hausdorff.

Let $G$ be a locally compact topological group, and suppose $G$ is compactly generated, that is $G = \langle S \rangle$ for some compact subset $S \subseteq G$. (In case $G$ is discrete, this is equivalent to being finitely generated.) In this case, the word metric $d_S$ for $G$ with respect to $S$ is geodesically adapted (see \cite[Chapter~4B]{Cornulier--delaHarpe}) and enjoys properties akin to the word metric of a finitely generated group. Moreover, a similar theory of (coarse) geometry for $G$ is available; we refer the reader to \cite{Cornulier--delaHarpe} for details. We may then consider, as usual, the corresponding word length $\ell_S = d_S(-,\id)$, the distance to the identity $\id \in G$.

The \emph{conjugator length} $\cl(h,h')$ of two conjugate elements $h,h'\in G$ measures the length of a shortest possible conjugating element, that is,  
\[
\cl(h,h')\define \min \{ \ell_S(k) : khk^{-1}=h' \text{ in } G \}. 
\]
\begin{definition}
\label{def:conjugatorlengthfunction}
The \emph{conjugator length function} of the compactly generated, locally compact group $G$ (a priori with respect to the compact generating set $S$) is the map
\[
\CLF: \N \to \Z_{\geq 0}, \quad \CLF(n) \define \max\{ \cl(h,h') \, : \,  h\sim h', \, \text{ and } \, \ell_S(h)+\ell_S(h')\leq n\},
\]
where $\sim$ is the conjugation relation. 
We adopt the convention that $\CLF$ is constant equal to zero if $G$ is abelian. 
\end{definition}

Given nondecreasing functions $f,g : \N \to \Z_{\geq 0}$, we say that \emph{$g$ quasi-dominates $f$}, written $f \qdom g$, if there exists an increasing affine function $a : \Z_{\geq 0} \to \Z_{\geq 0}$ such that $f(n) \leq a\circ g\circ a(n)$ for all $n\in\N$. (By affine function on $\Z_{\geq 0}$ we mean a map $a(x)=Cx+D$ for some $C\in \N$ and $D\in\Z_{\geq 0}$. Note that $a(\N) \subseteq \N$.) If $f \qdom g$ and $g \qdom f$, we call $f$ and $g$ \emph{quasi-equivalent} and write $f \qeq g$. That $\qeq$ is an equivalence relation is an easy exercise. The \emph{growth rate} of $f$ is its equivalence class under $\qeq$, and we adopt common terminology. For instance, $f$ grows polynomially of degree $d$ in case $f\qeq n \mapsto n^d$, or exponentially when $f \qeq n \mapsto e^n$.

\begin{lemma}
Two conjugator length functions of a compactly generated locally compact group $G$, induced by two compact generating sets, are quasi-equivalent. 

In particular, the rate of growth of a conjugator length function for $G$ does not depend on the choice of compact generating set. 
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is immediate: 
if $S$, $S'$ are compact generating sets for $G$, then $(G,d_S)$ is bilipschitz equivalent to $(G,d_{S'})$ via the identity map; see \cite[Proposition~4.B.4(3)]{Cornulier--delaHarpe}.
\end{proof}

It should be clear that one needs a good understanding of the set of all elements $w\in G$ conjugating a given $h$ to a given $h'$. We call this set the \emph{coconjugation set} from $h$ to $h'$ and denote it by $\mathrm{C}_G(h,h')$. 
This allows us to write 
\[
\cl(h,h') = \min \{ \ell_S(k) \, : \, k\in\mathrm{C}_G(h,h') \}. 
\]
We shall look at those sets in \Cref{sec:coconjugation-sets}.


