\subsection{The Moore--Penrose pseudo-inverse}
\label{sec:pseudoinverse}

Along the proof of \Cref{thm:lineargrowth} we will have to find minimum-norm solutions to systems of equations, so let us recall some key concepts.

Let $Ax=b$ be a system of linear equations with real coefficients. Thus for some $m,n \in\N$ the letter $A$ denotes a real $m\times n$ matrix, $b\in V=\R^m$, and $x=(x_1,\ldots, x_n)^{\mathtt{T}}$ a list of variables. As we know from linear algebra the set of solutions to this system of equations is an affine subspace $\LAb$ of $V$. 
In case $A$ is invertible the unique solution is given by $A^{-1}b$. In all other cases, the solutions are not unique and $A$ is not invertible. 
To find a minimum-norm solution to the system $Ax=b$ in such cases, we need to compute the distance between the space $\LAb$ and the origin. 
This is achieved using a notion of a pseudoinverse. There are many (equivalent) ways to define it. We chose to follow \cite{CampbellMeyer} and Definition~1.1.3 therein. 

\begin{definition}[{Moore--Penrose pseudoinverse}]
\label{def:MoorePenrose}
	Let $A$ be an $m\times n$ matrix with real or complex entries. The \emph{pseudoinverse} $A^+$ of $A$ is the unique $n\times m$ matrix such that all of the following hold:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $AA^+A=A$, that is, $A^+$ maps all column vectors of $A$ onto themselves; 
		\item $A^+AA^+=A^+$, that is, $A^+$ has a weak inverse property; 
		\item $AA^+=(AA^+)^*$, that is, $AA^+$ is Hermitian; and 
		\item $A^+A=(A^+A)^*$, that is, $A^+A$ is also Hermitian.   
	\end{enumerate} 
\end{definition}

The pseudoinverse exists  
for any matrix and agrees with the usual inverse in case $n=m$ and $A$ is of full rank. 
For a proof of the next claim see \cite[Theorem 2.1.1]{CampbellMeyer}. 

\begin{prop}
\label{prop:pseudoinverse}
Let $A\in\R^{m\times n}$, $b\in\R^m$. Then $x_0\define A^+ b \in \R^n$ is the minimal least squares solution to the system of equations $Ax=b$. That is, $\| Ax_0-b \| \leq \| Ax- b\| $ for all $x\in \R^n$ and $x_0$ has smallest possible norm among all vectors satisfying this inequality.
\end{prop}


There are many methods to compute pseudoinverses, cf. \cite[Cap\'itulo~16]{ElonLinA}. We shall make use of the following, and refer the reader to \cite[Theorem~1.3.1]{CampbellMeyer} for a proof.

\begin{prop} 
	\label{prop:calculatingpseudoinverses}
Suppose $A\in\R^{n\times n}$ has rank $r$. Choose a basis $\{v_1,\ldots,v_r\}$ for the image and a basis $\{u_1,\ldots,u_{n-r}\}$ for the kernel of $A^\ast$, respectively. Consider the $n\times n$ matrices 
\[A_1 \define \left( \begin{smallmatrix} | & & | & | &  & | \\ v_1 & \cdots & v_r & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\ | & & | & | &  & | \end{smallmatrix} \right) \quad \text{ and } \quad A_2 \define \left( \begin{smallmatrix} | & & | & | &  & | \\ Av_1 & \cdots & Av_r & u_1 & \cdots & u_{n-r} \\ | & & | & | &  & | \end{smallmatrix} \right).\]
Then $A_2$ is invertible and moreover $A^+ = A_1 \cdot A_2^{-1}$.
\end{prop}

