\section{Model} \label{section:model}
\noindent In this section we give details on our model setup and introduce the WPF method. Let $\Xi$ be a closed subset of $\mathbb{R}^m$. We write $\mathfrak{P}(\Xi)$ for the set of probability distributions on $\Xi$ and $\pmProb_{\xi}$ for the point-mass distribution that assigns probability $1$ to $\xi \in\Xi$. %\note{In this case it seems better to write without the curly braces. Otherwise should be subset not in. EJA Agreed} 
All of the distributions considered in this paper are Borel probability measures. For a Polish metric space $(\Xi,d)$, the \emph{Wasserstein distance} \parencite{Optimal-Transport:Villani} between two distributions $\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q}\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi)$ is
\begin{equation*}
    W(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q}) \defeq \underset{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q})}{\inf}\int_{\Xi\times\Xi} d(\xi,\zeta) \,\drv\gamma(\xi,\zeta),
\end{equation*}
where $\Gamma(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q})$ is the set of all couplings of $\mathbb{P}$ and $\mathbb{Q}$; that is, the set of all probability distributions %EJA left this "prob distribution" on purpose
on $\Xi\times\Xi$ with first marginal $\mathbb{P}$ and second marginal $\mathbb{Q}$. We assume throughout the paper that the distance $d$ is lower semicontinuous which implies that the infimum is attained \parencite[Theorem~4.1]{Optimal-Transport:Villani}. %\edit{EJA: This does not rule out my idea to include discussion of a 'sticky' distance - since this gives a lower semi-continuous distance.} \edit{[Yes. We can eke out around half a percent more performance for dairy price prediction using this.]}

%\edit{[This plays nice with the parallels to Kalman but maybe we think about jut jumping ahead as "assuming" the proportionality can put people off?.]}

Suppose that there is a sequence of underlying distributions $\{\mathbb{P}_t \in \mathfrak{P}(\Xi)\}_{t\in[T]}$ generated from some unknown stochastic process. For some $\lambda \geq 0$ we suppose the likelihood of a shift from $\mathbb{P}_t$ to $\mathbb{P}_{t+1}$ is proportional to
\begin{equation*}
\exp\bigl(-\lambda W(\mathbb{P}_t, \mathbb{P}_{t+1})\bigr).
\end{equation*}
Under the assumption that shifts between periods are independent of the history of previous distributions, the likelihood of realising the sequence $\{\mathbb{P}_t\}_{t\in[T]}$ given $\mathbb{P}_1$ is proportional to
\begin{equation*}
    \prod_{t=1}^{T-1} \exp\bigl(-\lambda W(\mathbb{P}_t, \mathbb{P}_{t+1})\bigr).
\end{equation*}
Our discussion here is informal. We deal with likelihoods and so the relative probabilities are the key quantities. But to do this in a formal sense requires some version of a density on the infinite-dimensional space of $T$-tuples of distributions on $\Xi$, which is not straightforward.

For distributions $\mathbb{P}_1,\ldots,\mathbb{P}_T$, the likelihood of observing the outcomes $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T\in\Xi$ is the product $\prod_{t=1}^{T} {\mathbb{P}_t}(\{\xi_t\})$. Hence, the likelihood of realising the sequence $\{\mathbb{P}_t\}_{t\in[T]}$ given $\{\xi_t\}_{t\in[T]}$ is proportional to
\begin{equation*}
    \prod_{t=1}^{T} {\mathbb{P}_t}(\{\xi_t\})\prod_{t=1}^{T-1} \exp\bigl(-\lambda W(\mathbb{P}_t, \mathbb{P}_{t+1})\bigr).
\end{equation*}
Thus, for historical observations $\widehat{\xi}_1,\ldots,\widehat{\xi}_T\in\Xi$, we solve the problem %\note{moved subject to main set we draw from (defn) under maximize, maybe this looks worse. Will wait for approval before changing later. EJA I agree we should not have "subject to" for this type of definitional inclusion. But I prefer not to have an optimisation problem where the "maximize" is the verb in a sentence (it is harder to read), so I changed this. } 
\begin{alignat}{2}\label{problem:WPF}
    & \kern 0.12em \underset{\mathbb{P}_1,\ldots,\mathbb{P}_T\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi)}{\maximize} && ~~ \sum_{t=1}^{T} \log\bigl(\mathbb{P}_t(\{\widehat{\xi}_t\})\bigr) - \lambda \sum_{t=1}^{T-1} W(\mathbb{P}_t, \mathbb{P}_{t+1}) \tag{WPF} %\\
     %& \text{subject to} && \quad \mathbb{P}_t\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi) \quad \forall t \in[T]\notag
\end{alignat}
to obtain a maximum-likelihood estimate for the sequence of underlying distributions. This has the natural interpretation of simply maximizing log-likelihood with a penalty on changes in the distribution between time periods. Note that we may have $\mathbb{P}_t(\{\widehat{\xi}_t\}) = 0$, so in order for (\ref{problem:WPF}) to be well defined, throughout the paper we set $\log(0) \defeq -\infty$ and $\exp(-\infty) \defeq 0$. 

We also consider a version of the problem with more than one observation per time period. Suppose observation $\widehat{\xi}_t$ is made in period $s(t)\in[S]$, with $s(t)$ increasing in $t$ and at least one observations in each of the $S$ periods. Here $s(1)=1, \ldots, s(T)=S$, so for this version of the problem we simply replace $\mathbb{P}_t$ with $\mathbb{P}_{s(t)}$ in (\ref{problem:WPF}) to give the problem %\note{can't we just drop last blue?} \note{Okay, I guess the procedure is to use "to" active tense link to the maximise for the main optimization problem, and then the secondary problems forget this (so we keep the blue)}
\begin{alignat}{2}\label{problem:WPF-G}
    & \kern 0.12em \underset{\mathbb{P}_1,\ldots,\mathbb{P}_S\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi)}{\maximize} && ~~ \sum_{t=1}^{T} \log\bigl(\mathbb{P}_{s(t)}(\{\widehat{\xi}_t\})\bigr) - \lambda \sum_{t=1}^{T-1} W(\mathbb{P}_{s(t)}, \mathbb{P}_{s(t+1)}) \tag{TG-WPF}.% \\
     %& \text{subject to} &&  \quad \mathbb{P}_s\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi) \quad \forall s \in[S].\notag 
\end{alignat}
We call this the time-grouped version of WPF. Observe that this is the original problem with the additional constraint $\mathbb{P}_{t}=\mathbb{P}_{t+1}$ imposed whenever $s(t)=s(t+1)$.

\begin{remark}[Extreme values of $\lambda$]
Large values of $\lambda$ correspond to a large penalty on changes in the distribution between periods. If $\lambda = \infty$, then every distribution in the optimal solution to (\ref{problem:WPF}) is the empirical distribution on the observations. On the other hand, if there is no penalty on changes in the distribution between periods, so $\lambda = 0$, then the sequence of point-mass distributions on each observation is optimal.
\end{remark}

Through the definition of the Wasserstein distance, (\ref{problem:WPF}) has minimizations in its objective function. These terms are multiplied by $-\lambda \leq 0$, and thus we include them in the overall maximization. For a feasible solution to (\ref{problem:WPF}) with distributions $\Prob_1,\ldots,\Prob_T$, %unnecessary to give $\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi)$, 
since the infimum in the definition of the Wasserstein distance is attained (as assumed throughout), there exist consecutive minimal-cost transportation plans $\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_{T-1}\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi^2)$ between  $\Prob_1,\ldots,\Prob_T$. Applying the Gluing Lemma \parencite[Page~23]{Optimal-Transport:Villani}, it follows that a distribution $\liftedProb\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi^T)$ with associated marginals $\Prob_1,\ldots,\Prob_T$ and $\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_{T-1}$ exists. Let $\Indicator$ denote the event indicator; that is $\Indicator \{\cE\} =1$ if the event $\cE$ is true and $\Indicator \{\cE\}=0$ if it is false. Expressing $\log \bigl(\Prob_t(\{\widehat{\xi}_t\})\bigr)$ as $\log\bigl(\int_{\Xi^T} \Indicator\{\xi_t = \widehat{\xi}_t\}  \,\drv\liftedProb(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T)\bigr)$ and $W(\Prob_t,\Prob_{t+1})$ as $\int_{\Xi^T} d(\xi_t, \xi_{t+1}) \,\drv\liftedProb(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T)$ enables us to reformulate (\ref{problem:WPF}) as
\begin{alignat}{2}\label{problem:lifted-discrete-support}
    & \kern 0.12em \underset{\liftedProb\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi^T)}{\maximize} && ~~ \sum_{t=1}^{T} \log \biggl( \int_{\Xi^T} \Indicator\{\xi_t = \widehat{\xi}_t\}  \,\drv\liftedProb(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T) \biggr) - \lambda \sum_{t=1}^{T-1} \int_{\Xi^T} d(\xi_t, \xi_{t+1}) \,\drv\liftedProb(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T).%\\
    %& \subjectTo && \quad \liftedProb \in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi^T).\notag
\end{alignat}
Here the distributions $\Prob_t$ in the original formulation are simply the marginals of $\liftedProb$.

\subsection*{Finite-Dimensional Reduction}
Optimizing over arbitrary probability distributions in $\mathfrak{P}(\Xi)$ is an infinite-dimensional problem.  
Our first result relies on the lower semicontinuity of the distance $d$ through the reformulation (\ref{problem:lifted-discrete-support}), and shows that no suboptimality is imposed by instead optimizing over distributions supported only at a finite number of points. Let $\mathfrak{P}_{\nu}(\Xi)\subset \mathfrak{P}(\Xi)$ denote the set of discrete probability distributions %left this "prob distribution" on purpose
supported on at most $\nu$ points within $\Xi$.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:discrete-support}
    For any feasible solution to (\ref{problem:WPF}), there exists a solution consisting of discrete distributions each supported on at most $T+1$ points with the same objective value. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $(\Prob_1,\ldots,\Prob_T)$ be a feasible solution to (\ref{problem:WPF}) and let $\liftedProb$ be a feasible solution to (\ref{problem:lifted-discrete-support}) with marginals $\Prob_1,\ldots,\Prob_T$. For this solution $\liftedProb$ the objective function of (\ref{problem:lifted-discrete-support}) features $T$ log terms and we suppose these have values $c_1,\ldots,c_T\in \Reals\cup\{-\infty\}$.  %\note{feels like we don't need say. EJA OK I have rewritten to avoid this.}. 
Moving these into $T$ constraints and taking exponents on each side, we deduce that by solving the problem
\begin{alignat}{2}\label{problem:lifted-problem-with-constraint-objective}
    & \kern 0.12em \underset{{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}}\in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi^T)}{\maximize} && \quad - \lambda \sum_{t=1}^{T-1} \int_{\Xi^T} d(\xi_t, \xi_{t+1}) \,\drv{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}}(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T) \\
    & \text{subject to} && \quad \int_{\Xi^T} \Indicator\{\xi_t = \widehat{\xi}_t\}  \,\drv{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}}(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T) = \exp(c_t) \quad \forall t\in[T],% \notag\\
    %& && \quad {\bm{\mathrm{Q}}} \in\mathfrak{P}(\Xi^T), \notag
\end{alignat}
we will obtain from the marginals a feasible solution to (\ref{problem:WPF}) that has the same objective value as $(\Prob_1,\ldots,\Prob_T)$. The problem (\ref{problem:lifted-problem-with-constraint-objective}) is an infinite-dimensional linear program with $T$ equality constraints in the space of Borel probability measures on $\Xi^T$, and, consequently, it has an optimal solution that is a discrete distribution supported on at most $T+1$ points in $\Xi^T$; see \parencite[Appendix~B]{yue2022linear} which uses a result of \textcite{pinelis-2016-extreme-points-of-moment-sets}. Thus, imposing the additional constraint $\bm{\mathrm{Q}}\in\mathfrak{P}_{T+1}(\Xi^T)$ results in no change to the optimal objective. The result follows. 
\end{proof}

Our next result makes use of the fact that the distance $d$ satisfies the triangle inequality and shows that no suboptimality is imposed by instead optimizing over distributions supported only on points within the set of observations.
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:discrete-support-on-observations}
For any feasible solution to (\ref{problem:WPF}), there exists a solution consisting of discrete distributions supported only on points within the set of observations with either the same or an improved objective value.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}%[Proof of Proposition~\ref{proposition:discrete-support-on-observations}]
We leverage Lemma~\ref{lemma:discrete-support} and consider the equivalent problem (\ref{problem:lifted-discrete-support}). Let $(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T)\in\Xi^T$ be a point satisfying $\xi_t \notin \{\widehat{\xi}_1,\ldots,\widehat{\xi}_T\}$ for some $t \in [T]$, and for a solution $\liftedProb\in\mathfrak{P}_{T+1}(\Xi^T)$ to (\ref{problem:lifted-discrete-support}) suppose that $\liftedProb\bigl(\{(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T)\}\bigr)>0$. Such an assignment of probability does not contribute to the $t^{\text{th}}$ log term in the objective function of  (\ref{problem:lifted-discrete-support}). Moreover, if $\xi_t \neq \xi_{t+1}$, then the sum of the distance terms in the objective function would not be increased by instead assigning the probability $\liftedProb\bigl(\{(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T)\}\bigr)$ to the alternate point $(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_{t-1},\xi_{t+1},\xi_{t+1},\ldots,\xi_T)\in\Xi^T$, since the triangle inequality implies that it would be at least as cheap to move directly from $\xi_{t-1}$ to $\xi_{t+1}$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
    d(\xi_{t-1},\xi_{t+1}) + d(\xi_{t+1},\xi_{t+1}) = d(\xi_{t-1},\xi_{t+1}) \leq d(\xi_{t-1},\xi_{t}) + d(\xi_{t},\xi_{t+1}).
\end{equation*} 
If on the other hand $\xi_t = \xi_{t+1}$, either there is some $s \geq 1$ such that $\xi_{t} = \cdots = \xi_{t+s} \neq \xi_{t+s+1}$ and the previous reasoning again applies, or $\xi_1 = \cdots = \xi_{T} \notin \{\widehat{\xi}_1,\ldots,\widehat{\xi}_T\}$ and clearly the probability $\liftedProb\bigl(\{(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_T)\}\bigr)$ can be assigned to $(\widehat{\xi}_1,\ldots,\widehat{\xi}_1)\in\Xi^T$ without worsening the objective value. In the case where $t=T$, we use a similar argument that instead compares $\xi_t$ and $\xi_{t-1},\ldots,\xi_1$. 
\end{proof}

