\section{Analysis of Components}\label{section:analysis-of-components}
%\edit{[Are the components are unaffected by the lack of uniqueness in $w$ ?] [EJA: Components are for any feasible solution. The results stated are correct I think.]}

%\edit{[words about identifying observations which are values in $\Xi$ with their indices which are the time at which they where observed.]}

The solution of the network-flow problem (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}) provides a natural grouping of historical observations based on the probability mass transported between certain subsets of the nodes in the network. In this section we study the implications of this grouping and provide structural results which characterise how the probabilities assigned to each observation are affected by the value of the observations and the periods in which they are observed.
%In what follows we identify observations with both their index and their numerical value which is possible since we assume that the numerical value of each observation is unique.

A feasible solution $w$ to (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}) defines a graph on the nodes $\{1,2, \ldots, T\}$ with arcs $(i,j)$ where either $w(i,j) >0$ or $w(j,i)>0$. We say that a \emph{component} $\mathcal{C} \subset [T]$ in a feasible solution $w$ is a component of this graph (i.e., a maximal subgraph in which a path between every two nodes exists). 


In the example of Figure~\ref{figure:example-network-solution} there are three components: The first component consists of observations~1, 3, and 6 ($6.13$, $6.47$, and $7.13$), the second component is just observation~2 ($7.85$) as a singleton, and the third component consists of observations~4 and 5 ($4.91$ and $5.54$). Within the first component the terminal distribution assigns no probability mass to observation $1$, and within the last component it assigns no probability mass to observation $4$.  

%\edit{[need to exclude the case where max flow on path is 1 or min is 0. I think the only case where this cannot occur at optimality is when there is only one component?][EJA: I agree this is the only case. It is Proposition 3 where the statement needs to be careful.]}

\subsection{Bounds on Component Weights}

It will be helpful to work with a path-based description of the network-flow version of WPF. We  consider all possible paths  $\cP_1,\ldots,\cP_L\subset[T]$ between the source and sink nodes, and write $(j,k) \in \cP_l$ if $j$ and $k$ appear as successive elements of $\cP_l$. We do not include the source $\{0\}$ or the sink  $\{T+1\}$ in the path. In this way a solution $w$ to (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}) is associated with a set of flows $x_1,\ldots,x_L\in[0,1]$ on these paths with 
$$w(j,k)=\sum_{i : (j,k) \in \cP_i} x_i.
$$  
By identifying the first element in each path we can also write $w(0,j)$ as a sum; that of $x_i$ over paths with $\{j\}$ as the first element. A similar process gives $w(k,T+1)$.
We write $D(\cP)$ for the total distance along a path $\cP$, so 
$$
D(\cP) =\sum_{(j,k) \in \cP}d(j,k),
$$ 
and we write $\cN (j) \subset [L]$ for the set of indices of paths that include node $j$. Thus, another way to state problem (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}) is
\begin{equation}\label{WPF-path}
\underset{x\in\Reals_+^L} {\maximize} \quad \sum_{j=1}^T \log \biggl(\,\sum_{i \in \cN(j)} x_i \biggl) -\lambda \sum_{i=1}^L x_i D(\cP_i) \quad \subjectTo \quad \sum_{i=1}^L x_i=1. 
\end{equation}
%\edit{[$\underset{x}{\maximize}$, $\subjectTo$..., this parenthing looks bad, either use tinysum or something else.  EJA Agreed, please change.]}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:path-derivative}
A feasible solution $w$ to (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}), with flow decomposition $x_1,\ldots,x_L$ over the set $\{\cP_1,\ldots,\cP_L \}$ of possible paths from source to sink, is optimal if and only if there exists a constant $\pathConstant \in \Reals$, such that, for every path $\cP_i =\{i_1,i_2, \ldots, i_k\}$  
\begin{equation} \label{equation:path-constant}
 \sum_{j=1}^{k}\frac{1}{\sum_{i \in N(i_j)} x_i}-\lambda D(\cP_i) \le \pathConstant,
\end{equation}
with equality if $x_i >0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The problem (\ref{WPF-path}) is a maximization of a concave objective function. Dualising the $\sum x_i =1$ constraint, there is a value $\pathConstant$ (which is the associated Lagrange multiplier) such that the optimal solution occurs when
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{j=1}^T \log \biggl(\,\sum_{i \in N(j)} x_i\biggr) -\lambda \sum_{i=1}^L x_i D(\cP_i)+\pathConstant \biggl(1-\sum_{i=1}^L x_i\biggr)
\end{equation*}
is maximized over $x_i \ge 0$. Taking derivatives, when $x_i > 0$, 
\begin{equation*}
 \sum_{j=1}^{k}\frac{1}{\sum_{i \in N(i_j)} x_i}-\lambda D(\cP_i) - \pathConstant =0,
\end{equation*}
and otherwise when $x_i = 0$, then the derivative is negative, as required. Sufficiency follows from the standard Karush--Kuhn--Tucker argument.
\end{proof}


For a component $\mathcal{C}$ in a solution to (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}) we write the total probability weight for the component as 
$$
H(\mathcal{C})=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{C}}w(0,i).
$$
%\edit{[Can't use $W$ here, maybe use $M$ and refer to mass rather than weight. But problems with $w$ vs $M$ and $m$ is dimension. Could use $r$ for dimension ($r,n,m$).  EJA I chose $H$. Happy to go with something else, but I think $M$ is a poor choice]}

Due to conservation of flow, this is equal to the sum of terminal probabilities on nodes in this component,  i.e., 
$$H(\mathcal{C})=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{C}}w(i,T+1)=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{C}}\mathbb{P}_T (\{\widehat{\xi}_i\}).$$ 

Our next result bounds the total probability weight on a component in terms of the number of observations 
%\edit{[it contains/it consist of / it is made up of?. EJA.  Happy with includes]}
that it includes.

\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:component-mass-upper-bound}
For each component $\cC$ in an optimal solution $w$ to (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}) the following bounds apply 
\begin{equation*}
   \frac{\lvert\cC\rvert}{\pathConstant + \lambda D_{\max}(\cC)} \leq H(\cC) \leq \frac{\lvert\cC\rvert} {\pathConstant},
\end{equation*}
where $\pathConstant$ is defined as in (\ref{equation:path-constant}) and $D_{\max}(\cC)=\max
\{D(\cP_i):\cP_i \subset \cC\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}

We proceed by taking an optimal solution $w$ to the network-flow problem (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow})  with a flow decomposition $x_1,\ldots,x_L\in[0,1]$ on the paths $\cP_1,\ldots,\cP_L\subset[T]$ between the source and sink. By the definition of  components, it is clear that no path flows include elements from more than one component.

For a given component $\cC$ we consider paths $\cS_1,\ldots,\cS_K$ in 
$\cC$ with flows $x_1,\ldots,x_K >0$. The total probability weight in this component is simply the sum of the flows: $ H(\cC) = \sum_{k=1}^K x_k$,
and thus we rewrite the result of Lemma \ref{lemma:path-derivative} as 
\begin{equation*}
 \sum_{j \in \cS_k}\frac{1}{\sum_{i : j \in \cS_i} x_i}-\lambda D(\cS_k) = \pathConstant,
\end{equation*}
for each path $\cS_k\in \cC$. Hence,
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:boundA}
 \sum_{k=1}^K \sum_{j \in \cS_k}\frac{x_k}{\sum_{i : j \in \cS_i} x_i} = \sum_{k=1}^K x_k (\pathConstant+\lambda D(\cS_k)).
\end{equation}
Now, the left-hand side of (\ref{eqn:boundA}) is simply the number of observations in the component, as
\begin{equation*}
 \sum_{k=1}^K \sum_{j \in \cS_k}\frac{x_k}{\sum_{i : j \in \cS_i} x_i} = \sum_{j \in \cC} \sum_{k : j \in \cS_k}\frac{x_k}{\sum_{i : j \in \cS_i} x_i}
 = \sum_{j \in \cC} \frac{1}{\sum_{i : j \in \cS_i} x_i}\sum_{k : j \in \cS_k}x_k = \lvert\cC\rvert.
\end{equation*}
Then, since $\pathConstant \le \pathConstant+\lambda D(\cS_k) \le \pathConstant + \lambda D_{\max}(\cC)$, the result follows from (\ref{eqn:boundA}).
\end{proof}

Suppose that $\{i\}$ is a component with only one element (a ``singleton''), which therefore consists of a single path $\cP=\{i\}$. The only flow through $i$ is from $0$ to $i$ to $T+1$. Since $D(\cP)=0$, Proposition~\ref{proposition:component-mass-upper-bound} implies $ \Prob(\{\widehat{\xi}_i\}) = 1/\pathConstant$. (Equality holds since $D_{\max}(\cC)=0$.)  Proposition~\ref{proposition:component-mass-upper-bound} further establishes that the total probability weight on any component $\cC$ is at most $\lvert\cC\rvert \cdot \Prob(\{\widehat{\xi}_i\})$, i.e.,  $\lvert\cC\rvert$ 
%\edit{[the number of observations][EJA why?]}
times as large as that for a singleton component with a single observation. We typically find that the observations which end up as singleton components are far away from any of the other observations and thus could be described as outliers. For instance, in Example~\ref{example:small-network} it is the observation $7.85$ that ends up as a singleton.  On the other hand, observations which are part of the same component are typically close together, and as a consequence of the previous reasoning, end up with a lower total probability weight than if they had been outliers. In effect, the WPF method gives more weight to outliers.

This relative upweighting of outliers is an unusual feature of the WPF method. The intuition here is that a set of observations that are close together are likely to occur as the result of a single point of mass in the underlying distribution that has shifted over time, whereas there is less likelihood that the mass responsible for the outlying observation has moved.

\subsection{Weights Weakly Decrease With the Age of an Observation.}

We expect that observations further in the past will have smaller weights attached to them.  This is true only in a weak sense. We will show that if the sequence of observations is changed so that an observation is moved backwards, with no other changes, and in addition the order of the observations in its component is left unchanged, then its weight will either stay the same or be reduced. The implication (by moving elements one at a time starting from the earliest) is that if a whole component is moved earlier then none of the elements can have its weight increased.

We will need some additional notation at this point. We consider an unordered set of observations $A_\xi=\{\xi_1, \xi_2, \ldots \xi_T \}$ and let $\sigma: \{1,2,\ldots , T \} \rightarrow A_\xi$ be the sequence in which these occur. Given a particular sequence $\sigma$ we are interested in the optimal final distribution $\mathbb{P}_T$ of probabilities attached to each of these observations, the optimal probability attached to observation $\xi_j$ under sequence $\sigma$ we write as $p^*(\sigma, \xi_j)$. We also write $C(\sigma, \xi_j)$ for the component containing $\xi_j$ in an optimal solution for the sequence $\sigma$.

Consider a particular observation $\sigma(i_0)$ which is in position $i_0$. We define a new sequence  $\tilde{\sigma}$ which swaps $i_0$ and $i_0-1$, thus $\tilde{\sigma}(i_0)=\sigma(i_0-1)$ and $\tilde{\sigma}(i_0-1)=\sigma(i_0)$ with other elements of $\tilde{\sigma }$ being the same as $\sigma$.

The result below shows that the weight on a particular  observation decreases or stays the same if it is swapped so that it appears one earlier in the sequence, provided that the observations involved are in different components. 

\begin{theorem} \label{backwards-swap}
If $i_0$ and $i_0-1$ are in different components in the optimal solution under sequence $\sigma$, with $\sigma(i_0)=\xi_j$ then $p^*(\sigma, \xi_j) \ge p^*(\tilde{\sigma}, \xi_j)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
First note that we can reformulate the problem by identifying the weights $w(i,j)$ with the observations rather than the sequence orders, so giving weights $w(\xi_i,\xi_j)$. Thus we can formulate the problem given a set of observations $A^+_\xi$ which is $A_\xi$ together with additional dummy observations $\xi_0$ and $\xi_{T+1}$, as follows:

\medskip
{\def\arraystretch{1.5} 
\begin{tabular}{ll}
maximize  & $ \Pi(w)=\sum_{j=1}^{T}\log (\sum_{i=0}^{T}w(\xi_i,\xi_j))-\lambda
\sum_{j=1}^{T}\sum_{i=0}^{T}w(\xi_i,\xi_j)d(\xi_i,\xi_j)$ \\ 
subject to & $\sum_{j=1}^{T}w(\xi_0,\xi_j)=1$ \\ 
& $\sum_{i=0}^{T}w(\xi_i,\xi_j)=\sum_{k=1}^{T+1}w(\xi_j,\xi_k)\quad \forall j \in [T]$ \\ 
& $w(\xi_0,\xi_{T+1})=0$ and $w(\xi_i,\xi_j)=0$ if $\sigma^{-1}(j)< \sigma^{-1}(i)$ \\
& $w(\xi_i,\xi_j)\geq 0$.
\end{tabular}
}
\medskip

The change from $\sigma$ to $\tilde{\sigma}$, leaves the problem unchanged except for the constraints on which elements $w(\xi_i,\xi_j)$ are zero. We suppose that $\sigma(i_0)=\xi_j$ and $\sigma(i_0-1)=\xi_k$. Then the constraint $w(\xi_j,\xi_k)=0$ is dropped and a new constraint $w(\xi_k,\xi_j)=0$ is added. 

We write $w^*$ for the optimal solution to the problem with $\sigma$ and $\tilde{w}^*$ for the new optimal solution with $\tilde{\sigma}$. Thus the Theorem statement can be written $\tilde{w}^*(\xi_j,\xi_{T+1}) \le w^*(\xi_j,\xi_{T+1})$. 

Define   $v(\xi_i,\xi_k)=\tilde{w}^*(\xi_i,\xi_k)-w^*(\xi_i,\xi_k)$ for every $\xi_i,\xi_k \in A^+_\xi$. We will suppose that $v(\xi_j,\xi_{T+1})=\delta >0$ and find a contradiction.

Since $i_0-1$ is in a different component to $i_0$ we know that $w^*(\xi_k,\xi_j)=0$. This implies that $w^*$ is feasible for the problem with $\tilde{\sigma}$. Because $w^* \ne \tilde{w}^*$ the uniqueness of the optimal solution $\tilde{w}^*$ for the problem with $\tilde{\sigma}$ implies $\Pi(w^*)<\Pi(\tilde{w}^*)$. Since $w^*$ is optimal for the problem with $\sigma$ we must have $\tilde{w}^*$ infeasible for the original problem and thus $\tilde{w}^*(\xi_j,\xi_k) >0$ and since $w^*(\xi_j,\xi_k)=0$ by the constraint, we have $v(\xi_j,\xi_k)>0$. 

We create a positive flow $v^+$ by reversing any negative elements of $v$. Thus we set $v^+(\xi_i,\xi_k) = -v(\xi_k,\xi_i)$ and $v^+(\xi_k,\xi_i)=0$ whenever $v(\xi_k,\xi_i)<0$. It is easy to see from the feasibility of $w^*$ and $\tilde{w}^*$ for their respective problems that $v$ satisfies 
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=0}^{T+1} \left( v(\xi_i,\xi_j)-v(\xi_j,\xi_i) \right)=0 \quad \forall j\in {0}\cup [T+1].
\end{equation*}
Moreover the same is true for the flow $v^+$ which is thus a circulation, i.e. having conservation of flow at all nodes.  

By the standard result on decomposition of flows we decompose this into flow cycles, and let $v_1$ be the sum of all flow cycles including the arc $(\xi_j,\xi_{T+1})$. Then $v_1(\xi_j,\xi_k)=0$ since any cycle including the arc  $(\xi_j,\xi_{T+1})$ cannot also include the arc $(\xi_j,\xi_k)$ in that direction. 

Now observe that $v_1$ is a feasible variation to $w^*$ for the $\sigma$ problem, and since $w^*$ is optimal $\Pi(w^*+v_1) < \Pi(w^*)$. The strict inequality follows because otherwise we have two optimal solutions and we get a contradiction from Theorem \ref{theorem:unique-solution}. Also if $v_2=v^{+}-v_1$ is the sum of all the cycles in $v^{+}$ not including the arc $(\xi_j,\xi_{T+1})$, we have $\Pi(w^*+v_2) \le \Pi(w^*+v^{+})=\Pi(\tilde{w}^*)$. In these expressions if there are flows in both directions on an arc then the smaller is netted off the larger to produce a single flow.  

Notice from the standard greedy approach to creating a decomposition of the flow that all the cycles are of flows that on any given arc have the same direction as the original flow $v^{+}$. We begin with the network version in which each observation node $\xi_i$ is split into an ``in'' node $\xi_i^{\text{in}}$ and an ``out'' node $\xi_i^{\text{out}}$ with a single ``observation'' arc $(\xi_i^{\text{in}},\xi_i^{\text{out}})$ connecting them (so that the objective entirely consists of costs/benefits applying to arcs). 

Let the total change in distance for $v_1$ be $D_1$, and for $v_2$ be $D_2$.  Let the total flow in the arc $(\xi_i^{\text{in}},\xi_i^{\text{out}})$ be $w^*_i$ under $w^*$, $a_i$ under $v_1$ and $b_i$ under $v_2$. Notice that where $\tilde{w}^*$ is less than $w^*$ on an observation arc $i$ then both $a_i$ and $b_i$ are negative, and where $\tilde{w}^*$ is more than $w^*$ on observation arc $i$ then both $a_i$ and $b_i$ are positive.

Now we have
\begin{align*}
 \Pi(w^*+v_1) - \Pi(w^*) & = \sum \log (w^*_i +a_i)-\sum \log(w^*_i) - \lambda D_1 \\
 & \ge \sum \log (w^*_i +a_i+b_i)- \lambda D_1 - \lambda D_2 - \sum \log(w^*_i+b_i) + \lambda D_2 \\
& = \Pi(w^*+v_1+v_2) - \Pi(w^*+v_2),
\end{align*}
where the inequality follows from concavity of the log function applied at each observation point making use of the fact that $a_i$ and $b_i$ cannot be of opposite signs. This contradicts our observation that $\Pi(w^*+v_1) - \Pi(w^*) < 0$ and $\Pi(w^*+v) - \Pi(w^*+v_2) \ge 0.$
\end{proof}

\section{Numerical Results}\label{section:numerical-results}
%\note{Check consistent probability notation. EJA I have changed the notation to line up with what we had earlier}

\noindent In this section we evaluate the performance of the WPF method on price-forecasting and portfolio-optimization applications. Historical observations are taken from multidimensional real-world data sets collected over long timescales, and we use a parameter-tuning scheme which could be employed in practice. As benchmarks, we also compare to the performance of sample average approximation, windowing, and simple exponential smoothing. Like WPF, these all define weighted empirical distributions on the historical observations. Sample average approximation sets 
{$$p_T(1) =1/T,~\ldots,~p_T(T) = 1/T,$$} windowing for window size $s \in [T]$ sets {$$p_T(T) = 1/s,~\ldots,~p_T(T-s+1) = 1/s,~p_T(T-s) = 0,~\ldots,~p_T(1) = 0,$$} and smoothing for decay rate {$\alpha\in (0,1]$ sets {$$p_T(T)=\bar{\alpha},~p_T(T-1)=\bar{\alpha}(1-\alpha),~p_T(T-2)=\bar{\alpha}(1-\alpha)^2,~\ldots,~p_T(1)=\bar{\alpha}(1-\alpha)^T.$$} Here $\bar{\alpha}=\alpha/\bigl(1-(1-\alpha)^{T+1}\bigr)$ is chosen so that the probabilities sum to $1$.}

To evaluate performance we adopt a training/testing approach, splitting time-series data into a training set (the first 70\,\%) and a testing set (the remaining 30\,\%). In the training phase decisions are not made during an initial warm-up period while information is collected. 
%\note{EJA. My reading of this suggests that the warm up period is irrelevant, so I suggest removing its mention. No decision will have an impact before a time = beginning of testing minus two years. Am I wrong about this?} \note{Yes, the only reason for it is to "collect information" so we have a reasonable amount of observations to begin with. I guess not important.} \note{EJA Leave for now - but I think it is confusing}
After this, decisions are made sequentially, incurring costs based on the subsequent observation. These costs inform parameter tuning in the testing phase, where at each time step the method selects the parameter that minimizes the total cost over a fixed parameter tuning window into the past.

In the testing phase, decisions continue to be made using all prior observations, with parameters updated dynamically based on performance in the parameter tuning window. This window introduces a trade-off: a longer window yields more stable parameter estimates, while a shorter window responds more quickly to changes in dynamics. In our evaluations we found that a two year tuning window worked well for WPF, and that varying this window had little effect on the benchmarks. Note that in this training/testing set up, decision making and parameter tuning rely solely on data collected in the past, i.e., the tests are consistent with how this method could be applied in practice \parencite{tashman-2000-out-of-sample-forecasting}.

Our evaluations of the WPF method use the exponential-conic reformulation of (\ref{problem:reduced-network-flow}), implemented in \texttt{Julia} \parencite{Julia} with \texttt{JuMP.jl} \parencite{JuMP} and \texttt{MathOptInterface.jl} \parencite{mathoptinterface}, and solved with \texttt{COPT} \parencite{COPT}. Here distances are measured using the metrics induced by the $1$-, $2$-, and $\infty$-norms, which we denote by $L_1$, $L_2$, and $L_{\infty}$, respectively.

