\subsection{Price Forecasting}
The international prices of dairy commodities are influenced by factors such as global economic conditions and consumer demand, both of which evolve over time. Forecasts of such prices inform the production planning of dairy companies around the world. Figure~\ref{figure:dairy-prices-data} graphs fourteen years of monthly Global Dairy Trade (GDT) prices for Anhydrous Milk Fat (AMF), Butter (BUT), Butter Milk Powder (BMP), Skim Milk Powder (SMP), and Whole Milk Powder (WMP).\footnote{Data provided by Dowson \parencite{dairy-forecasting}.} The GDT price is the average per-unit price of each product sold at international commodity auctions in a given month.
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centerline{
    \hspace*{1.7cm}{\includegraphics[width=16cm]{figures/dairy-prices.pdf}}}
    \caption{\textbf{Historical GDT prices.} Monthly observations from June 2010 to May 2024.}
    \label{figure:dairy-prices-data}
\end{figure}
\noindent Figure~\ref{figure:dairy-prices-data} shows that the GDT prices of each product are correlated. In particular, the prices of the fat products (AMF and BUT) and the milk powders (BMP, SMP, and WMP) are coupled throughout the fourteen-year period, with a change in behaviour around $2016$.

To forecast future GDT prices, we use a weighted linear regression on the log prices. For log-prices $\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_T \in \Reals^m$ observed at times $t=1,\ldots,T$, assigning the pairs $(\ell_1,\ell_2),\ldots,(\ell_{T-1},\ell_T)$ probabilities $p_1,\ldots,p_{T-1}$, the log price at time $T+1$ is forecast as $\mu^{\star} + A^{\star} \ell_T$, where $\mu^{\star}$ and $A^{\star}$ are the optimal solutions to the problem
\begin{equation*}
    \underset{\mu\in\Reals^{m},A\in\Reals^{m\times m}}{\minimize} ~~ \sum_{t=1}^{T-1} p_t\bigl\lVert\ell_{t+1}-(\mu+A\ell_t)\bigr\rVert^2_2.
\end{equation*}
(Note that this has an analytic solution; see, e.g., \parencite[\S~11.5]{seber-lee:Linear-Regression-Analysis}.) When the next log price $\ell_{T+1}$ is realised, we incur the cost $\bigl\lVert\ell_{T+1}-(\mu^{\star}+A^{\star}\ell_T)\bigr\rVert^2_2$. The weighted regression approach here allows adaptation to shifting log-price dynamics.

