\subsection*{Appendix: An Example Without Stability}



Consider the following example. We take $\lambda=4$. The sequence of $\widehat{\xi}_i$ values are 
$$
0,0,0,0,0,1,-1,-1,\underline{-1},1,1,1,-1,1,1,\underline{1},-1,-1,-1,1, \ldots ,-1,-1,\underline{-1},1,1,1,-1, \ldots .
$$
This is defined as five zeros followed by a sequence of $1$ and $-1$ values, that is split into alternating groups: within each group there is a repeated pattern, either three $-1$ values and then one $1$ value, or the reverse. It will turn out that this pattern provides a sufficient local mixture between $1$ and $-1$ that it is never worthwhile to include a path which jumps from $1$ to $-1$ or vice versa. The groups are of increasing length with a switch to the opposite group when the total number of observations of $-1$ are 50\,\% more than the number of observations of $1$, or vice versa. The first three times this switch occurs are at $t=9$, $t=16$ and $t=31$ (shown underlined in the list above). But it is clear that the reversals will happen infinitely often. Our aim is to show that as each observation is added to the sequence the optimal solution will be unstable, with no limiting value for the weight assigned to the first nonzero observation, $\widehat{\xi}_6$. 

We first establish that in a path decomposition of the optimal solution there is no value of $T>20$ for which a path with a transition from $1$ to $-1$ or vice versa can occur with nonzero weight. This transition is most likely to occur right at the end. Suppose that the final four observations are $\widehat{\xi}_{T-3}=1$, $\widehat{\xi}_{T-2}=\widehat{\xi}_{T-1}=\widehat{\xi}_{T}=-1$ and the value of $\mathbb{P}_{T-3}(\{1\})=x$ with all the other weight at $-1$ (there will be none at $0$). The condition that is needed to include a new path which has $w(T-2,T-1)>0$ is that the derivative along this new path is greater than $0$ since the alternative is to have $w(T-2,T+1)=x$. In other words we require 
$$
3\frac{1}{1-x}-2\lambda>0
$$
i.e. $x>0.625$.  We will show that for $T>20$ both $x$ and $1-x$ are less than $0.625$ and hence that there is no path of this form. 

We may thus consider solutions which make use of just 4 paths (with weights $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$). $\cP_1$ goes from the source to the first observation at $1$, and then moves between successive observations at $1$. $\cP_2$ goes from the source to  $\widehat{\xi}_1$ at $0$ then stays at $0$ for the first five observations until it moves to the first observation at $1$, before following $\cP_1$. $\cP_3$ and $\cP_4$ match $\cP_1$ and $\cP_2$ but use observations at $-1$ rather than $1$. If there are a total of $n_A$ observations at $1$, and $n_B$ observations at $-1$ then we can show that an optimal solution has $x_1=x_3=0$, $x_2=n_A/(n_A+n_B)$ and $x_3=n_B/(n_A+n_B)$. This is done using Lemma \ref{lemma:path-derivative}. We take $\pathConstant=5+n_A+n_B-\lambda$ and then check that (\ref{equation:path-constant}) holds with equality for paths $\cP_2$ and $\cP_3$ and the inequality holds for $\cP_1$ and $\cP_4$ since $\lambda<5$.  Now observe that by construction we have $x_2$ varying between $1.5 n_B/(2.5n_B)=0.6$ and $n_A/(2.5n_A)=0.4$. These are approximate expressions - we switch between groups as soon as the ratio exceeds 3/2, but the accuracy increases as $T$ gets larger. So this all works for $T$ large enough. For smaller values of $T$ we may not meet the requirement for both $x_2$ and $x_3=1-x_2$ to be less than $0.625$. With this example we have $x_2=0.25$ at $T=9$ and $x_2=0.636$ at $T=16$, but at $T=31$ we get $x_2=16/26=0.615$ which meets our requirement.  

Thus we have established that for large enough $T$ the probability $\mathbb{P}_6(\{\widehat{\xi}_6\})$ will oscillate between approximately 0.4 and 0.6 and have no limiting value as successive new observations are made and  $T \rightarrow \infty$. 


