\section{Interactive Python Interface to CTDSE (PyI-CTDE)}
\label{Interactive Python Interface to CTDSE}
The TDSE code can be also individually accessed through a~Pythonic interface relying on the Ctypes library, which provides access to low-level c-functions. The low-level computational routines are compiled from the same source as in the computational pipeline of the whole model, Subsection~\ref{User Interfaces: computational pipeline}. An arbitrary input electric field can be used in PyI-CTDSE, which provides a~user-friendly and flexible way to study the microscopic physics. Additionally to the solver itself, various analysing routines for the TDSE output, such as computation, of the photoelectron spectrum, Gabor transform,~\dots, and the time-dependent wavefunction, are available as well. % The full description of the implemented C-analyses available directly from Python is available in the description of the \texttt{TDSE\_DLL} within the respective documentation of 1D-TDSE~\cite{git}.

An example of usage is in Section~\ref{Physical examples: MIcroscopic insights}, more details about the implementation together with the full list of implemented C-analyses available directly from Python are directly in the documentation of the 1D-TDSE module~\cite{Vabek_Multiscale_modular_approach_2025}. One of more advanced analyses is the time-dependent electron energy density.





\subsection{Analysing the Time-Dependent Electron Energy Density}
\label{Analyzing the Time-Dependent Electron Density}
One of the tools for providing a~detailed microscopic insight is the photoelectron spectrum~\cite{Catoire2012}, which provides the energetic distribution of electrons after the interaction with the pulse. The structure includes, for example, above-threshold ionisation peaks, which are important to understand multiphoton processes. A~special care is needed to extend this methodology to get insight into the process during the interaction because of the gauge freedom in the description of the interaction. We adopt the invariant methodology introduced in~\cite{Vabek2022} (please find there the details about the treatment of the gauges). The resulting time-dependent energy distribution is
\begin{align}
		\mathcal{P}(E,t) = \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0}\braket{\psi(t)|R_{H_0}(\varepsilon)|\psi(t)} \,,
		&&
		R_{H_0}(\varepsilon) = N_{\varepsilon}\varepsilon^2((E-H_0)^2 + \varepsilon^2)^{-1}\,,
\end{align}
where $\ket{\psi(t)}$ is expressed in the length gauge, $R_{H_0}(\varepsilon)$~is the resolvent operator corresponding to the field-free part of the Hamiltonian~\eqref{eq:Microscopic Response in XUV regime (TDSE): Hamiltonian (a)} and $N_\varepsilon$ is a~normalisation factor the specific for a~given numerical representation. It is convenient to compute\footnote{Because $H_0$~is the only non-trivial operator in the expression, there is no commutativity issue and we can use $A^2+B^2=(A+\cu B)(A-\cu B)$.}
\begin{align}
		\braket{\psi(t)|\varepsilon^2((E-H_0)^2 + \varepsilon^2)^{-1}|\psi(t)}
	=
		\left\lVert ((E-H_0) - \cu \varepsilon)^{-1}\psi(t) \right\rVert^2 \,,
\end{align}
where the inversion is done consistently with the numerical solver~\eqref{eq:1D-TSDE Implementation (CTDSE):CN - step 2}.


\section{Physics Examples \& tutorials}
\label{Physics Examples}
Now, we present various features of the code suite by showing them in sample physical scenarios. We start with typical environments: a~gas cell (Subsection~\ref{Physical examples: HHG in a Gas-Cell}) and gas jet with a~Gaussian density profile (Subsection~\ref{Physical examples: HHG in a Gas-Jet}). Next, we move to more customised cases. We show the generation in a~pre-ionised gas (Subsection~\ref{Physical examples: Absorption-limited HHG with Pre-Ionization in a Gas-Cell}), which is based on a~previous physical application of the code suite~\cite{xxx}. We show how to implement user-provided input pulses (Subsection~\ref{Physical examples: HHG with Bessel-Gauss Beams}). Finally, we show the microscopic analysis of the time-dependent electron density (Subsection~\ref{Analyzing the Time-Dependent Electron Density}).

Note that the main purpose of this section is to demonstrate the current capabilities of the code and typical accessible characteristics. Each of the following examples is treated in more details in a~respective tutorial implemented in a~jupyter notebook where more details are present.

\subsection{HHG in a Gas-Cell}
\label{Physical examples: HHG in a Gas-Cell}
The default option to run the code is a~Gaussian beam and pulse entering a~gas cell with a~homogeneous density. To present this basic mode of operation, we consider a~Gaussian 30-fs laser pulse (defined by the intensity dropping to $1/\e^2$ of the peak focus intensity) with the waist $w_0=100~\mathrm{\mu m}$. The peak intensity is set to approximately $2.44\times 10^{14}~\mathrm{W/cm^2}$, which corresponds to the extent of the harmonic plateau up to the 40th harmonic according to the classical cut-off law, $E_{\text{cut-off}}=I_p+3.17U_p$~\cite{Corkum1993}. Because $E_{\text{cut-off}}$ is linearly proportional to the intensity through~$U_p$, it provides a~convenient way to measure the intensity, which we adapt in our examples. Then we place a~1-cm-long gas cell filled by argon at 25~mbar in the path of the beam keeping the original focus in the middle of the cell.

Figure~\ref{fig:PE_cell_pulse} shows the 1)~distortion of the pulse due to the non-linear propagation and, 2)~the corresponding degrees of ionisation. The pulse at the exit of the cell, Fig.~\ref{fig:PE_cell_pulse}b), exhibits the reduction of the peak intensity due to defocusing and the redistribution of the intensity into the off-axis "wings" of the pulse. This behaviour is explained by the plasma defocusing: The free electrons, Fig.~\ref{fig:PE_cell_pulse}c), reduces the refractive index in the region with the free electrons. This effectively forms a~diverging lens for the subsequent portion of the pulse.

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig/cell/cell1.png}
\caption{The initially Gaussian pulse in time~$t$ and radial coordinate~$\rho$ expressed in the units of harmonic cut-off in panel~a) propagates through 1-cm-long 25-mbar argon gas cell leading to a~distorted pulse~b). The bottom panels~c,d) shows the respective plasma profiles.}
\label{fig:PE_cell_pulse}
\end{figure}

The next steps of the pipeline compute all the dipoles induced in the macroscopic volume and then integrates the produced XUV signal via the means of the Hankel transform. The harmonic spectra are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:PE_cell_spectra}. The code can store--for a~reference--the transforms of the entry and exit planes. As the entry pulse is Gaussian both in space and time, the entry-plane transform in Fig.~\ref{fig:PE_cell_spectra}a) contains typical ring structures (cf.~Fig.~8 of~\cite{Catoire2016}). The result of the interest for applications is the integrated harmonic spectrum shown in different scales in Figs.~\ref{fig:PE_cell_spectra}c,d).

In summary, this shows the simple mode of operation showing the visualisations of the usual quantities of interest.

