\section{Introduction}
\label{intro}

Large language models (LLMs) are an effective machine learning tool that can perform a broad range of reasoning tasks, such as mathematical problem solving, code generation, commonsense inference, and strategic board games. However, their ability to operate effectively in real-time, multi-agent coordination settings remains under-explored~\cite{han2012game}. Given the rise of multi-agent systems, characterized by dynamic collaboration and automation, there is a need for new frameworks capable of supporting real-time strategic adaptation. Although game theory offers a robust mathematical framework for modeling strategic interactions, it typically assumes fixed decision rules that lack the flexibility to accommodate real-world complexity and change~\cite{han2012game}. In contrast, reinforcement learning (RL) provides a flexible mechanism for learning through experience; however, it requires extensive training and often produces strategies that lack clear interpretability. To guide this investigation, we ask the following research question: \textit{How can LLMs dynamically adapt and refine their game strategies based on real-time feedback in multi-agent, multi-turn environments to achieve enhanced rationality and equilibrium outcomes?}


A number of prior works have~\cite{HeNegotiation2018, LewisBargaining2017, Toolformer2023, PaLME2023, ReAct2022, NegotiationArena2022, StarCraft2023, CiceroDiplomacy, CAMEL2023, AutoGen2023, LangAgent2023, Voyager} studied negotiation, tool integration, real-time planning, and embodied reasoning in the context of LLM-based decision-making. For example, He et al.~\cite{HeNegotiation2018} and Lewis et al.~\cite{LewisBargaining2017} explore end-to-end learning for negotiation dialogues, while Toolformer~\cite{Toolformer2023} demonstrates how LLMs can autonomously learn to invoke external tools during generation. PaLM-E~\cite{PaLME2023} highlights the value of integrating visual and proprioceptive inputs for grounded multimodal reasoning. ReAct prompting~\cite{ReAct2022} illustrates how models can reason and act by interleaving tool calls with intermediate steps. NegotiationArena~\cite{NegotiationArena2022} and StarCraft II benchmarks~\cite{StarCraft2023} provide multi-agent coordination evaluations, and CICERO~\cite{CiceroDiplomacy} showcases strategic interaction through dialogue in high-stakes, turn-based environments. Recent frameworks such as CAMEL~\cite{CAMEL2023}, AutoGen~\cite{AutoGen2023}, LangAgent~\cite{LangAgent2023}, and Voyager~\cite{Voyager} have introduced collaborative LLM teams with structured roles and planning objectives.

Other solutions such as SWE-agent~\cite{SWEAgent2023} and MetaGPT~\cite{MetaGPT2023} propose programming-based role assignment and skill composition pipelines, yet often operate with fixed dialogue states and precomputed coordination graphs. OctoAgents~\cite{OctoAgents2024} explore zero-shot LLM team formation but require architectural homogeneity and lack continual learning mechanisms. The MindAct~\cite{MindAct2024} and CoLLM~\cite{CoLLM2023} models examine collaboration over temporal chains but still assume a known world model. While prior solutions~\cite{HeNegotiation2018, LewisBargaining2017, Toolformer2023, PaLME2023, ReAct2022, NegotiationArena2022, StarCraft2023, CiceroDiplomacy, CAMEL2023, AutoGen2023, LangAgent2023, Voyager} enable symbolic reasoning, tool usage, and basic interagent dialogue, they typically do not support continuous strategic adaptation in cooperative, real-time environments. Most of these existing approaches operate in static settings or rely on single-shot policy inference. There is little ability for agents to revise goals, respond to degraded teammate performance, or restructure plans in response to unforeseen environment transitions. A central open challenge is, thus, how to enable agents to adjust their strategy mid-execution using context-aware feedback, memory persistence, symbolic priors, and feedback-aware control loops that reflect real-time game-theoretic dynamics.

An examination of the literature that addresses uncertainty, partial observability, and strategic adaptation in games reveals several key limitations~\cite{GTBench, GameTheoryLLM, RationalLLM, Voyager, EmergentComm1, EmergentComm2, EmergentComm3, CiceroDiplomacy, RLHF}. GTBench~\cite{GTBench} tests LLMs on game-theoretic matrix tasks, finding poor robustness in partially observable environments. GameTheoryLLM~\cite{GameTheoryLLM} formalizes Bayesian agent workflows for negotiation but imposes synchronous update protocols. RationalLLM~\cite{RationalLLM} studies equilibrium behavior but does not support belief evolution or policy trajectory tracking. MARL benchmarks like MAgent~\cite{MAgent2018} and EnvAgent~\cite{EnvAgent2023} test emergent roles but fail to align agents with structured symbolic constraints. Systems like MindAgent~\cite{MindAgent2024}, DEAM~\cite{DEAM2024}, and ToM-GPT~\cite{ToMGPT2024} simulate theory-of-mind and belief graphs but rely on heavily curated scenarios. CoaLLM~\cite{CoaLLM2023} and OpenAgents~\cite{OpenAgents2023} attempt to unify self-play and LLM planning, though they still assume pre-scripted task flows.

Although CICERO~\cite{CiceroDiplomacy} offers a rich symbolic policy in the context of Diplomacy, it is limited to turn-based play. Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)~\cite{RLHF} allows reward shaping but lacks dynamic revision based on environment evolution. Existing frameworks such as like RolePlay~\cite{RolePlay2023} and ThoughtAgents~\cite{ThoughtAgents2024} aim to inject personality and dialogue abstraction but do not connect memory or belief state with performance feedback. Few systems track and utilize contextual priors across time steps. Even fewer are capable of aligning their behavioral revisions with task-level metrics like regret, coordination efficiency, or Nash deviation. We argue that bridging this gap requires dynamic feedback systems that are tightly coupled with symbolic modeling and game-theoretic planning principles. The field still lacks a unified method for enabling LLM-based agents to revise their strategies in real-time as goals shift, agents deviate, or coordination structures deteriorate.

The strategic use of LLMs in dynamic, real-time, multi-agent environments faces a significant open problem: how to enable agents to reason adaptively, coordinate under uncertainty, and revise decisions as environments evolve. While early systems~\cite{GTBench, GameTheoryLLM, RationalLLM, Voyager, EmergentComm1, EmergentComm2, CiceroDiplomacy} have made progress in specific areas, such as static equilibrium modeling, symbolic dialogue protocols, or role-based planning, they remain limited in three key aspects: 1) they are unable to revise beliefs in response to shifting goals or noisy conditions, 2) they lack real-time mechanisms for coordination across agents, and 3) they do not incorporate symbolic priors or game-theoretic principles into feedback loops. These limitations highlight the need for integrated multi-agent reasoning frameworks that support real-time strategy adaptation and structured coordination, guided by symbolic feedback and contextual understanding under uncertainty.





The main contribution of this paper is to address the limitations of static decision-making methods, including rule-based strategies, turn-based reasoning, and offline reinforcement learning, by proposing a new framework that enables agents to adapt strategies in real time using LLM-guided reward shaping. By integrating symbolic evaluations from a frozen language model with a reinforcement learning loop, the proposed system refines agent behavior based on contextual signals such as order progress and timestep. This hybrid framework is designed to support collaboration in unpredictable, noisy environments where conventional policies tend to fail. In addition to developing the architecture, the proposed framework is empirically evaluated across dynamic environments to assess performance, robustness to noise, and latency tradeoffs. The proposed method delivers strategic benefits while preserving real-time inference speeds. In summary, the key contributions of this paper are:

\begin{itemize}
\item We assess the performance of our framework over the Overcooked-AI~\cite{wang2020overcooked} simulation environment. In these simulations, across five dynamic, partially observable, and high-noise environments, we design and implement a real-time shaping framework that connects proximal policy optimization (PPO)-based agents with frozen LLM evaluations. By leveraging prompt-based symbolic feedback during execution, the framework enables agents to revise decisions mid-episode in response to uncertainty, teammate variability, and timing disruptions. Agents generate structured prompts that encode state, goals, and local history, and use returned judgments to shape the reward signal in a latency-constrained control loop.

  \item We formalize symbolic feedback in multi-agent coordination by adapting game-theoretic principles such as belief revision, strategic consistency, and role alignment into a reinforcement learning-compatible reward-shaping pipeline. This symbolic layer guides agents toward cooperative behaviors by enforcing priors on mutual belief, future intent, and role commitment, without requiring full observability or centralized policy access.

\item Across five randomized, high-noise environments, including joint observation noise and delayed timing inputs, the results show that the proposed PPO-LLM agents achieved up to a 26\% improvement in average episodic return compared to PPO-only baselines. These gains were robust across different seeds and included rare successful completions under adversarial disruptions, where baseline agents consistently failed.

  \item In terms of real-time performance, PPO-LLM agents operated with an average per-action latency of 1.03 to 1.05 milliseconds, inclusive of prompt generation and LLM inference. This satisfies Overcooked-AI’s latency thresholds and confirms the framework’s suitability for deployment in real-time multi-agent infrastructures with frequent decision cycles.

  \item PPO-LLM agents also demonstrated a 35\% higher full-task completion rate under noise, reflecting more reliable long-horizon coordination and better adaptation to mid-task disruptions. This includes recovery from failed pickups, bottlenecked transitions, and role misassignments under noisy state transitions.

  \item The proposed approach allowed the agents to achieve a 22.7\% reduction in average idle steps across evaluation episodes, indicating smoother temporal coordination, less waiting, and tighter synchronization between role-dependent sub-tasks. This reflects better implicit communication and increased responsiveness to evolving task conditions.

  \item Finally, PPO-LLM agents consistently converged to Nash-like equilibria in cooperative settings such as the Burger Kitchen scenario. Our results in this scenario show that the agents will adopt stable joint policies where deviations became suboptimal. This emergent equilibrium was achieved using symbolic feedback alone, without explicit joint training, parameter sharing, or centralized supervision.
\end{itemize}


The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents the system architecture, including the real-time shaping pipeline, symbolic feedback loop, and PPO policy integration. Section 3 provides empirical results from Overcooked-AI environments, including performance under noise, task completion rate, latency tradeoffs, and convergence to equilibrium. Section 4 concludes with a summary of findings, limitations, and directions for future research on real-time strategic adaptation using language-based feedback.






\section{System Architecture}
\begin{figure*}[t!]
\vspace{-0.15in} % Slight adjustment upwards
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Model_architecture.jpg}
  \caption{\textbf{Real-Time Shaping System with PPO + Frozen LLM.} The environment emits a grid-state at each timestep. That grid is \textit{flattened} into a 1D observation vector and passed to both the PPO agent and a prompt generator. In parallel, a \textit{prompt generator} encodes task context (e.g., remaining orders and step count) into a text prompt. This prompt is evaluated by a frozen LLM (GPT-Neo 1.3B), whose binary "good"/"bad" signal is mapped to a shaping bonus via the reward-shaping module. The PPO agent then updates its policy using this \textit{shaped} reward signal.}
  \label{fig:architecture}
\vspace{-0.20in} % Slight adjustment downwards
\end{figure*}



To support dynamic strategy adaptation in cooperative multi-agent environments, we introduce a real-time reward shaping architecture that integrates PPO with feedback from a frozen LLM. This system is designed to enhance agent coordination through symbolic reasoning and structured evaluations, without modifying the LLM weights or retraining during execution. It consists of six interconnected modules that operate simultaneously during each timestep: i) environment state emitter, ii) observation preprocessor, iii) PPO policy network, iv) prompt generation module, v) frozen LLM evaluator, and vi) reward shaping unit, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}.

At time \( t \), the environment emits a grid state \( s_t \), which represents the spatial layout of \( N = 2 \) agents, interactive objects (such as ingredients and plates), and task-relevant regions (such as delivery counters). Each grid cell encodes the type and state of any entity occupying it. This state is transformed into a one-dimensional observation vector to ensure compatibility with both the PPO policy network and the LLM prompt generator. We instantiate this structure using the Overcooked-AI framework~\cite{wang2020overcooked}, a cooperative multi-agent simulator in which agents must coordinate to cook and deliver meals under time pressure and spatial constraints. Figure~\ref{fig:overcooked_ai} illustrates our specific “Burger Kitchen” configuration, where one agent prepares cooked patties while the other assembles and delivers finished burgers. This layout introduces strict temporal dependencies and spatial bottlenecks that require dynamic role allocation and real-time strategic coordination.

The PPO agent, implemented using Stable-Baselines3~\cite{raffin2021stable}, takes the observation and computes an action \( a_t \) based on its learned policy \( \pi_\theta(a_t \mid s_t) \), where \( \theta \) represents the parameters of the neural network. The policy maps observed features to a discrete set of agent behaviors, such as movement or object interaction.

This action is then executed in the environment, which returns a scalar reward \( r_t \) and the next state \( s_{t+1} \). In parallel, a prompt generation module extracts key contextual signals, such as the number of remaining orders and the current timestep, and formats them into a text prompt (e.g., \texttt{"orders:2 t:25/400"}).

This textual prompt is evaluated by a frozen large language model (GPT-Neo 1.3B). The LLM produces logits for specific tokens such as \texttt{"good"} and \texttt{"bad"}, which reflect the model’s confidence that the agents are cooperating effectively. If the logit for \texttt{"good"} exceeds that for \texttt{"bad"}, a scalar bonus \( \lambda \) is applied:

\vspace{-0.09in}
\begin{equation}
\lambda = 
\begin{cases}
\lambda_{\text{bonus}}, & \text{if } \text{logit}(\texttt{"good"}) > \text{logit}(\texttt{"bad"}) \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:llm_shaping}
\end{equation}
\vspace{-0.09in}

If the LLM provides a positive evaluation, the agent's reward becomes:

\vspace{-0.09in}
\begin{equation}
r'_t = r_t + \lambda.
\label{eq:shaped_reward}
\end{equation}
\vspace{-0.09in}

Otherwise, the original reward \( r_t \) is retained. The shaping coefficient \( \lambda \) is manually tuned to remain small and bounded, ensuring stability during PPO training.

The shaped reward \( r'_t \) is used to compute the advantage function \( \hat{A}_t \), which guides PPO policy updates. We adopt the concept of generalized advantage estimation (GAE)~\cite{raffin2021stable}, which links short-term reward shaping with long-horizon credit assignment. The temporal difference error is defined as:

\begin{equation}
\delta_t = r'_t + \gamma V(s_{t+1}) - V(s_t).
\label{eq:td_error}
\end{equation}

Here, \( \gamma \) is the discount factor, and \( V(s) \) is the learned value function for state \( s \).

\begin{equation}
\hat{A}_t = \delta_t + (\gamma \lambda) \delta_{t+1} + (\gamma \lambda)^2 \delta_{t+2} + \cdots + (\gamma \lambda)^{T - t + 1} \delta_{T-1}.
\label{eq:gae}
\end{equation}



Because the LLM remains frozen, its evaluations are deterministic and repeatable. These evaluations instantiate two fundamental game-theoretic principles. The first is \textit{belief revision}, which parallels Bayesian updating: the LLM adjusts its cooperative judgment in response to evolving prompt content that reflects the current environment state, such as the number of pending orders, role distribution, or elapsed time. This mechanism allows the agent to revise expectations mid-task without retraining. The second principle is \textit{strategic consistency}, which is implemented by reinforcing only those actions that align with collaborative success. Agents receive positive shaping signals only when their behavior contributes meaningfully to shared objectives, thereby discouraging selfish or unstable strategies and encouraging long-term alignment.

To operationalize these principles, we embed structured symbolic evaluation into the reinforcement learning loop. A prompt generation module continuously encodes task-relevant context, such as pending order count, current timestep, and relative agent positions, which is passed to a frozen LLM that produces a binary cooperative judgment. This symbolic signal is then mapped to a scalar bonus and integrated into each agent’s PPO training pipeline in real time. By providing feedback on the overall quality of collaboration, rather than low-level action success, the framework guides agents toward strategic behaviors that prioritize task flow, anticipation of partner actions, and recovery from errors. This allows for dynamic behavioral adjustments during execution, even in the presence of degraded perception or unpredictable task disruptions. As a result, agents adapt their policies mid-episode using high-level, interpretable feedback. This architecture bridges low-level reinforcement learning with symbolic reasoning, enabling robust coordination, adaptive role assignment, and resilience to uncertainty, including observation noise, partial observability, and real-time variability.



By linking symbolic judgment to policy adaptation, the framework naturally enforces game-theoretic alignment over time. Both PPO agents respond independently to the same symbolic signal and adapt their strategies to maximize cooperative feedback. As training progresses, the agents converge toward a stable configuration in which each agent’s role complements the other. This convergence satisfies the key condition of a Nash equilibrium between two agents: once both agents reach their equilibrium strategies, neither can improve their outcome by deviating unilaterally. Importantly, the framework does not solve for equilibrium analytically. Instead, equilibrium-like behavior emerges through iterative feedback and reward shaping, without any need for direct coordination or access to the co-agent's internal state.

In summary, we have developed a new real-time shaping framework that integrates symbolic evaluation from frozen LLMs with policy optimization, enabling agents to adapt strategies dynamically in cooperative multi-agent settings. By fusing high-level symbolic reasoning with low-level reinforcement learning, our framework allows agents to revise behavior in real time without the need for retraining, message-passing, or centralized control. This approach offers several key advantages: (1) it provides interpretable, prompt-driven feedback that shapes coordination decisions at each step; (2) it enables agents to handle uncertainty, teammate variability, and perceptual noise through context-aware shaping; and (3) it facilitates convergence to equilibrium strategies via reward modulation rather than direct policy manipulation. The architecture grounds strategic behavior in game-theoretic principles such as belief revision and role alignment, supporting robust adaptation in dynamic environments. These properties make the system broadly applicable to settings where policies must remain flexible, resilient, and efficient under changing cooperative demands. We now evaluate its performance through a series of controlled simulations.


