\section{Simulation Results and Analysis}


We evaluate our framework in the Overcooked-AI simulator\cite{wang2020overcooked}, a cooperative multi-agent benchmark in which two agents must complete cooking tasks under time pressure and partial observability. In our custom burger kitchen scenario (Figure~\ref{fig:overcooked_ai}), Agent A must wait while the patty cooks, then hand it off to Agent B for assembly and delivery. This layout enforces role specialization, timing dependencies, and dynamic coordination challenges that test the ability of our PPO+LLM system to adapt strategies based on real-time symbolic feedback.

As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:overcooked_ai}, the symbolic reward shaping framework is instantiated in this environment, where agents coordinate over spatially separated roles. By linking symbolic judgment to policy adaptation, the framework gradually enforces game-theoretic alignment. Both PPO agents respond independently to the same symbolic signal and adapt their behavior to maximize cooperative feedback. Over time, they converge toward a stable configuration in which each agent’s role complements the other.

This emergent structure satisfies the core property of a Nash equilibrium between two agents: once both adopt equilibrium strategies, neither can improve its outcome by deviating unilaterally. Importantly, the framework does not solve for equilibrium analytically. Instead, this behavior emerges through iterative learning guided by symbolic feedback, without requiring direct coordination or access to the co-agent’s internal state.


