\subsection{Returns per Episode}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Env_Order_timing.png}
    \caption{
    \textbf{Burger Kitchen: Coordination Under Order Timing Noise.} Left: PPO+LLM agents adapt to temporal perturbations and successfully coordinate task flow despite misaligned order signals. The tomato icon represents an off-timing ingredient request, which the symbolic feedback loop helps agents recover from. Right: PPO baseline agents fail to deliver the completed burger due to miscoordination, resulting in idle output and a missed delivery. This figure illustrates how symbolic feedback enables robust strategy adaptation under timing noise.
    }
    \label{fig:env_order_timing}
\end{figure}



Figure~\ref{fig:bar_graphs_clean} summarizes average returns per episode for PPO and PPO+LLM. In the Clean setting, where agents observe the environment without any disturbance, PPO fails to achieve any reward. This shows that the agent fails to develop cooperative strategies without step-by-step feedback guiding its actions during the episode. However, PPO+LLM consistently has a return of 9.8 with zero variance, demonstrating that binary LLM evaluations are sufficient to support reliable task completion.

Under the Visibility Noise condition, where the agent’s input state is interrupted with a random amount of perceptual noise, PPO again collapses to a return of 0. This outcome shows PPO's sensitivity to low-level visual disturbances. In contrast, PPO+LLM stays strong and achieves a perfect return of 9.8 again. Since it learns from prompt-driven evaluations that do not rely on precise raw data, PPO+LLM remains stable even when perception is inconsistent.

The shaping mechanism becomes even more impactful under task-level disruption. In the Order Timing Noise setting, where task arrivals are randomly delayed or reordered, PPO performs poorly and has an average return of $-40.4$ (standard deviation 3.7). This exposes a lack of stable learning which leads to low-quality actions. PPO+LLM significantly mitigates this degradation, recovering over 10 points (mean: $-30.3$, std: 4.4). By consistently evaluating overall task progression (e.g., timestep and pending orders), the LLM provides structure that mitigates the unstable time dynamics that PPO alone fails to handle.

This pattern stays the same in the Combined Noise condition, where both visibility and timing are altered. PPO deteriorates to a mean of $-39.9$ (std: 3.6), while PPO+LLM recovers nearly 25\% of that loss with a mean return of $-29.6$ (std: 4.3). This shows how symbolic feedback helps learning when rewards are weak or inconsistent, by giving the agent a clearer understanding of progress.


