To evaluate coordination performance under temporal uncertainty, we test both PPO and PPO+LLM agents in the Order Timing Noise environment, where requested ingredients arrive at irregular intervals. This setting introduces misaligned task dependencies, increasing the risk of agents acting prematurely or stalling due to incomplete orders. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:env_order_timing}, PPO+LLM agents recover from off-cycle prompts (e.g., a tomato requested ahead of time) by dynamically reassigning roles and maintaining structured task flow. In contrast, PPO baseline agents lack a mechanism to reconcile timing inconsistencies, leading to delivery failures and idle outputs. This scenario highlights the effectiveness of prompt-based symbolic guidance in helping agents reason through dynamic task adjustments, enabling more stable coordination even when order streams are perturbed.



To evaluate robustness under perceptual uncertainty, we test agents in the \textit{Observation Noise} environment, where input states are partially masked or corrupted prior to reaching the policy network. This simulates scenarios in which agents must act with incomplete or degraded visibility. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:env_observation_noise}, PPO+LLM agents successfully maintain cooperative behavior, relying on symbolic evaluations that remain stable across noisy visual inputs. The frozen LLM reinforces high-level intent rather than low-level pixel accuracy, enabling agents to avoid collisions, infer teammate roles, and complete deliveries even when specific object placements are obscured. In contrast, PPO baseline agents often misinterpret the scene, leading to duplicated efforts or missed handoffs. This setting highlights the value of prompt-based feedback in providing consistent behavioral signals, especially when raw observations are unreliable.

To evaluate agent performance under simultaneous disturbances, we consider the Combined Noise environment, which introduces both observation corruption and task timing irregularities. This dual-source disruption challenges agents to interpret partially noisy visual inputs while also adjusting to asynchronous order arrivals. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:env_combined_noise}, PPO baseline agents frequently fail in this setting. Visual distortion causes hesitation or miscoordination, and mistimed ingredient requests result in broken task flow, leading to idle or undelivered meals. In contrast, PPO+LLM agents use symbolic feedback to remain adaptive. The LLM reinforces cooperative behavior even under perceptual ambiguity, enabling agents to reassess roles and recover from misalignments. This robustness allows PPO+LLM to maintain productive collaboration despite multiple overlapping sources of uncertainty, demonstrating the effectiveness of language-based shaping in complex, real-time environments.









\subsection{Robustness to Noise}
Figure~\ref{fig:bar_graphs_noise} shows the average return per episode under task-level noise. We evaluate each method's sensitivity to structured disturbances to determine its robustness. To simulate insufficient state visibility, visibility noise modifies the grid-level tensor prior to it reaching the policy network. PPO collapses under noisy conditions due to its dependence on unal tered input encodings for fine-grained value learning, resulting in no reward accumulation. In contrast, PPO+LLM maintains positive returns across both conditions. This demonstrates that symbolic evaluations from the LLM offer resilience by reinforcing cooperative intent, even when perception is corrupted or incomplete.

Since PPO+LLM's prompt-based evaluation relies on higher-level elements like order count and timestep, which are not directly harmed by visual corruption, it remains unaffected and keeps an average return of 9.8.

Order Timing Noise introduces uncertainty into the environment’s causal structure by changing when new objectives are presented. This disrupts the routine coordination patterns PPO often overfits to. Without the ability to anticipate task sequences, PPO’s policy collapses. PPO+LLM, however, keeps its strategic framework intact by relying on ongoing LLM-guided assessments to judge whether progress is being made. Its behavior remains adaptive, shaped by a reward signal that is context-aware and flexible.

Combined Noise, which is arguably the most difficult condition, introduces both perceptual distortion and goal-timing irregularity, disrupting low-level coordination patterns. PPO’s performance deteriorates substantially in this setting. In contrast, PPO+LLM continues to produce reliably higher returns by leveraging symbolic feedback from LLM prompts. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:bar_graphs_noise}, this feedback serves as an external corrective mechanism, helping agents focus on high-level cooperation rather than brittle stimulus-response loops. This enables more resilient coordination even when the environment becomes unpredictable or misaligned with learned routines.


