It is worth mentioning that a configuration $c$ of a cellular automaton is an application $c:\mathbb{Z}\to \mathcal{S}$ that specifies the state of each cell in a cell grid. The set of all possible configurations of a grid is represented by $\mathcal{C}$. If $c$ is a constant function, which would lead all cells to the same state, we call it \textit{trivial configuration}. The local rules applied in the neighborhood of a cell is a dynamical system that is called the  \textbf{global transition function} $G:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{C}$, where $G(c) =e$ is a new setting in $\mathcal{C}$. The group of CAs in which, $n=1$ and $|\s|=2$ (e.g., $\s = \{0,1\}$)  are called \textit{Elementary Cellular Automata (ECA)}.
