Finally, we show the stability of Algorithm \ref{alg:K}.
According to Theorem \ref{th:K_k}, $K(0,T)$ can also be derived using a ``Combination'' method, 
in which $K^{(i)}(0,T)$ is learned by Algorithm \ref{alg:mu_r} and $K(0,T)$ is combined through \eqref{equ:K_k}.
In contrast, Algorithm \ref{alg:K} learns $K(0,T)$ as a whole in the multi-asset financial market.
In Figure \ref{fig:K}, we compare the performance of these two methods.
In subfigure (a), 
%we assume that the true value of $\Sigma^{-1}$ is known.
%For $K(0, T)$, the two methods show nearly identical learning trends, demonstrating their effectiveness under the ``omniscient'' condition.
%(The convergence curve in the left subfigure is very similar to that in Figure \ref{fig:convergence}. 
